dec1c696cab647de0c2d4ebedc","7eb2a002fa5a94b5512cff12a6b52f04","8b8c67538fddcbf84c8abf85182c59fd","70b1fe7249d57c8231468c674fdb27f3","9be9a9d854f7ff22439dcf0424548d03","92350bbb1b2279b0cafb451bccdddd35","08222f2bd7180dadc2ae70e09b4825b1","4e4bcdf64d98b57edf02d304686dc67f","0ba4c069c7e5603763c80542836f601e","af54a733f82e94c6d8c5c3690a5a4561","f4d86aaae348b217874e957440b679d8","dcc18a81fa432d50606e8bfb6fe01ca0","451734f2161373548753549b5b154b0d","6e6aa91daeb5555b2e722648c2b487ae","4342d2835e7f7a48c040210fedfe71f5","6863b8039b7a4aec04869ff7f3123c43","faf65cb0e7137d68d0f7bf19716f3fc0","be960e4b5be5cf3e232911396848a613","e6ceb9a99c04bab706ba4a5df2ca7b23","06fda31128d1966cc2a48f71ec0a8fad","58860080d77819e5098c0e107386c83a","109699a52fe23af84d0d776e8e65ec5d","2e312365b5762a9adacbfcd0698f6928","20a4f9c3ebec3cad9d2d2cdaac312630","dd0007ddbe9708090dfe4ace63c8f2d1","adfbad502989ac18d19c4f344775eae0","220fc85e08d7d74aaccde868005f1af7","020935040750cc7f733bb78fb82b27f2","258503ab63c32ca662a8f20a04506963","8266ba75f7d9f8cc1ae96538c310984d","c2b821764689ddb153382698c70138f0"],"embeddings":null,"documents":["https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/\n\nAcademics | Degree Programs & Majors | CBU Home > Academics Academics For more than 75 years, CBU has been committed to developing bold, relevant academic programs that prepare students to thrive. Whether you’re just beginning your college journey, completing your degree or taking the next step with advanced studies to fuel your career, you’ll find your path at CBU. Apply Now In This Section In This Section Discover and Live Your Purpose. You’re not just seeking a career but a deeper, more meaningful purpose. You aim to impact and serve your community and beyond, uplifting others through your actions and faith. California Baptist University embraces your drive and uniqueness. Connect with your vocation in a Christ-led environment intent on supporting your interests, spirituality and career goals. Find Your Program Envision your influence and your place in this world leading and serving others in Christ’s image. CBU highlights the path ahead through academically rigorous, hands-on programs across engineering, education, business, healthcare, behavioral science,","https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/\n\nspirituality and career goals. Find Your Program Envision your influence and your place in this world leading and serving others in Christ’s image. CBU highlights the path ahead through academically rigorous, hands-on programs across engineering, education, business, healthcare, behavioral science, aviation, the visual and performing arts and more. Search by Keyword Submit Undergraduate Graduate Online New Programs for Fall 2026 Athletics Administration and Coaching, MS Biotechnology, BS Business Analytics, MS Doctor of Marriage and Family Therapy (Online) Exercise Physiology, MS Integrated Science, BS Music Education, MM Performance and Pedagogy, BM School Counseling Credential Schools & Colleges No two journeys are identical. You may be contemplating your role in the world or have reached a stage where you can empower others through your skills, talents and beliefs. CBU’s schools and colleges encourage your academic and spiritual exploration through small class sizes, Christ-centered education, supportive faculty and access to real-world opportunities. College of Architecture, Visual Arts and Design College of Arts and Sciences College of Behavioral and Social Science Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering College of Health Science College of Nursing Dr. Robert K. Jabs School of Business School of Christian Ministries Dr. Bonnie G. Metcalf School of Education Shelby and Ferne Collinsworth School of Performing Arts Next Steps Request Information Visit Campus How to Apply ©","https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/\n\nAdmissions & Aid Home > Admissions & Aid Admissions & Aid You’ve been created for a unique purpose. CBU makes it our mission to help you realize your role as a servant leader through a combination of faith-rooted academic programs, a Christian community invested in your success and financial support. Apply Now In This Section In This Section Get Started Live your purpose as you look ahead to what’s next. Explore all resources to understand the application and financial aid process, or contact an advisor for assistance. Traditional Undergraduate You’re making the transition from high school — or, you’re seeking out a new, invigorating community to complete your bachelor’s degree. CBU welcomes you — your drive, your inquisitiveness and your desire to explore in a faith-centered community. As a top private Christian University in Southern California, graduating students are ready to serve locally and globally, equipped to embody Christ’s principles through their giftings and vocation. We strive to see you succeed and connect with your career, regardless if you’re still deciding on a major of if you have your","https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/\n\nready to serve locally and globally, equipped to embody Christ’s principles through their giftings and vocation. We strive to see you succeed and connect with your career, regardless if you’re still deciding on a major of if you have your future precisely planned out. Review requirements for incoming freshmen and transfer students . Learn more about how to apply and explore scholarships for undergraduates . Graduate You aim to make a larger impact through your life in the various settings you live and work. Now, with the opportunity to lead, you seek further educational investment to advance your knowledge to provide this level of guidance. Get the direction needed to fulfill your vocation at CBU, whatever your aspirations or career. We consider where you are and structure our graduate programs around the schedules of working adults. To make this change, review all application requirements , and explore ways to pay for your degree . International We live in an increasingly global society. Today’s leaders not only need to think strategically and have a defined vision but operate compassionately and cross-culturally, forging connections in all contexts. CBU prepares our students not only to function in but set the pace of our dynamic society. We strive to nourish the whole student and fuel their success — through challenging","https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/\n\noperate compassionately and cross-culturally, forging connections in all contexts. CBU prepares our students not only to function in but set the pace of our dynamic society. We strive to nourish the whole student and fuel their success — through challenging academic programs and a mission to make a lasting impact. Think ahead to where you see yourself and the steps needed to get there. To get started, review requirements for international undergraduate and graduate students as well as financial aid options and other resources for starting your journey. Online Earn your degree wherever you happen to be. Perhaps you’re looking to finish your bachelor’s or you’re interested in moving up in your field. Pursue your purpose in a format and pace suiting your goals through one of CBU’s 40 online programs spanning bachelor’s, master’s and doctoral degrees. Attend full or part time with classes scheduled around your existing career and other obligations. Review all requirements before submitting an application, or reach out to our team with any questions. Financial Aid Resources Tuition & Aid Scholarships How to Apply for Aid Contact Financial Aid I feel very blessed that the thing that I love to do most in life also happens to","https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/\n\nout to our team with any questions. Financial Aid Resources Tuition & Aid Scholarships How to Apply for Aid Contact Financial Aid I feel very blessed that the thing that I love to do most in life also happens to be the thing that I am best at and am fortunate enough to make my living doing. Greg Johnson '08 B.M. in Theory and Composition My classes with Dr. Prather helped me decide that airport operations was the career path that I wanted to take. Now that I have been working in the industry, I do not regret that decision one bit. Dillon Chado '17 B.S. in Aviation Management CBU's nursing program was awesome because it had the latest and greatest technology that optimized learning and had clinical sites in high acuity facilities. ... CBU helped prepare me for the future as a registered nurse in the U.S. Navy. Haley Chambers '22 Nursing, B.S.N. Fulfill Your Purpose Request Information Visit Campus How to Apply ©","https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/\n\nLife at CBU Home > Life at CBU Life on Campus Pursue personal excellence and spiritual growth through CBU’s programs, clubs/organizations and support services geared toward every stage of your journey. Take a Tour In This Section In This Section Guiding the Whole Person Academics are but one part of the CBU experience. Whether you intend to live on campus or commute, CBU focuses on the whole student — your spiritual foundation, your commitment toward others and your curiosity to learn and grow. This approach starts with orientation and new student programs easing your transition to campus life, from clubs and activities where you’ll meet and forge connections with like-minded yet diverse peers to opportunities like our International Service Project for assisting others while reflecting Christ’s teachings. Ways to Get Involved Athletics Lance Up! The Lancers show the power of perseverance. Since CBU transitioned to an NCAA Division I school, we’ve brought home multiple regional and national championship wins. Check game schedules to cheer on our teams from the 5,050-seat Events Center. Learn More Special Programs Empowering Your Success Supporting success means nurturing the whole person right from the start of their journey. We foster this through programming for new students, our Center for Career and Professional Development and other resources meant to shepherd your personal,","https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/\n\nEmpowering Your Success Supporting success means nurturing the whole person right from the start of their journey. We foster this through programming for new students, our Center for Career and Professional Development and other resources meant to shepherd your personal, academic and spiritual development. Learn More Campus Activities Serve, Support & Lead Uncover your community and develop lifelong bonds — be it with others pursuing a similar field or through Bible study groups, cultural activities and local outreach efforts meant to engage your faith and help you understand your vocation. Learn More Living on Campus Dive into our dynamic, close-knit and passionate on-campus community, where you’re encouraged to support your peers and connect with others through your faith and interests. Housing See where you can live on campus — from traditional dorm-style accommodations to our new Magnolia Crossing apartments — before you apply for housing. Dining Nourish your tastebuds and social connections. Wherever you choose to dine, CBU welcomes you over a variety of healthy choices to sit down and create — or continue to build — lasting relationships with your peers. Facilities Study and relax at our Community Life and Commuter Lounges, or work toward a new fitness","https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/\n\nyou over a variety of healthy choices to sit down and create — or continue to build — lasting relationships with your peers. Facilities Study and relax at our Community Life and Commuter Lounges, or work toward a new fitness goal at the CBU Recreation Center. Explore all on-campus facilities. Student Support Services Investing in the whole person means accommodating all individuals. From the time your journey begins, we help you establish a firm academic foundation and offer various forms of assistance to guide you to the next stage, explore various careers or manage a personal crisis without interrupting your education. Academic Advising & Resources Disability Services Student Services Student Care Explore Our Campus Get to know our campus located in the heart of Riverside’s Magnolia Center neighborhood, from its facilities and housing to its faith-driven culture ready to embrace and guide your path forward. Start with a virtual tour or schedule a visit today. Virtual Tour Schedule a Visit ©","https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/\n\nAbout CBU Home > About About California Baptist University, one of the top Christian Colleges in the nation, is a private Christian university located near Los Angeles in Southern California. In This Section In This Section California Baptist University believes each person has been created for a unique purpose. CBU strives to help students understand and engage this purpose by providing a Christ-centered educational experience that integrates academics with spiritual and social development opportunities. Graduates are challenged to become individuals whose skills, integrity and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish them in the workplace and in the world. California Baptist University is one of the top private Christian colleges and universities in Southern California. CBU offers associate's, bachelor's, master's, doctoral and","https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/\n\nintegrity and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish them in the workplace and in the world. California Baptist University is one of the top private Christian colleges and universities in Southern California. CBU offers associate's, bachelor's, master's, doctoral and credential programs in Riverside and online. State of the University 2025 LOCATION: Riverside, California FOUNDED BY: Los Angeles Southern Baptist Association RELIGIOUS AFFILIATION: California Southern Baptist Convention RECOGNITION: 2026 U.S. News & World Report Best Regional University Accreditations Western Association of Schools and Colleges California Commission on Teacher Credentialing Board of Behavioral Science Examiners Accreditation Council for Business Schools and Programs National Association of Schools of Music State of California Board of Registered Nursing Commission on Accreditation of Athletic Training Education Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education Board of Registered Nurses American Council for Construction Education CBU’s bachelor degree programs in civil engineering (BSCE), electrical and computer engineering (BSECE) and mechanical engineering (BSME) have been accredited by the Engineering Accreditation Commission of ABET, http://www.abet.org . Key Facts 2 Associate's Programs 51 Master's Programs 162 Acres 1950 Year Founded 116 Bachelor's Programs 10 Doctoral Programs 12,516 Enrollment 100% Christian Faculty and Staff A Beautiful Campus Our beautiful 162-acre main campus is situated in the heart of Riverside's historic Magnolia Avenue district just a short drive from popular beaches and mountain resorts.","https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/\n\nPrograms 10 Doctoral Programs 12,516 Enrollment 100% Christian Faculty and Staff A Beautiful Campus Our beautiful 162-acre main campus is situated in the heart of Riverside's historic Magnolia Avenue district just a short drive from popular beaches and mountain resorts. The campus maintains the charm of the mission revival style while incorporating modern, technologically advanced facilities. The most recent architectural additions to campus are the 5,050-seat Events Center and the four-story apartment complex, Magnolia Crossing. Tour Campus A Division I Athletics Program California Baptist University began a successful transition to NCAA Division I after winning its first-ever Learfield Directors’ Cup in 2018. CBU's Lancers athletic program is gaining recognition as one of the best in the nation with numerous regional and national championship teams in a variety of sports. Visit CBU Athletics A Spiritual Mission In addition to community involvement through a variety of service projects and activities, California Baptist University also supports a wide range of highly active international programs. The CBU International Service Projects program annually sends scores of student teams throughout the world to teach and participate in recreation, construction and infrastructure. Visit Spiritual Life CBU in the Region CBU has established a reputation of excellence throughout the Inland Empire and beyond. School districts, law enforcement agencies, hospitals, churches and businesses seek","https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/\n\nworld to teach and participate in recreation, construction and infrastructure. Visit Spiritual Life CBU in the Region CBU has established a reputation of excellence throughout the Inland Empire and beyond. School districts, law enforcement agencies, hospitals, churches and businesses seek out and hold CBU graduates in high regard for their unique combination of competence, confidence and compassion. View Things Near CBU ©","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nCollege of Engineering Home > Academics > Schools and Colleges > College of Engineering College of Engineering The Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering at California Baptist University is a top Christian engineering college in California and is growing rapidly in terms of both students and facilities. Study the number one undergraduate background for Fortune 500 companies by discipline — engineering. Engineers have changed the course of world history and been prominent in missions efforts. View Programs The Department of Aviation Science, within the College of Engineering, is a world-class, state-of-the-art aviation program graduating aviation professionals who think critically, communicate effectively and lead ethically with excellence built on a strong Christian foundation. Learn More Study engineering at a Christ-centered, learning community The mission of the College of Engineering is to prepare engineering students of excellence and character, with a Christian worldview, who are called to serve, equipped to lead","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nstrong Christian foundation. Learn More Study engineering at a Christ-centered, learning community The mission of the College of Engineering is to prepare engineering students of excellence and character, with a Christian worldview, who are called to serve, equipped to lead and sent to engage the world with their lives and the appropriate use of technology. Find My Program Programs Aerospace Engineering Minor Aviation Dispatch Minor Aviation Flight, BS Aviation Management Minor Aviation Management, BS Biomechanics and Rehabilitation Engineering Minor Biomedical Engineering, BSBME Chemical Engineering Minor Chemical Engineering, BSChE Civil Engineering, BSCE Computer Engineering Minor Computer Science Minor Computer Science, BCS Computer Science, MSCS Construction Management Minor Construction Management, BS Data Science Minor Data Science, BS Electrical and Computer Engineering, BSECE Electrical Engineering Minor Engineering, BSE Environmental Engineering Minor Industrial and Systems Engineering Minor Mechanical Engineering Minor Mechanical Engineering, BSME Mechatronics Minor Missionary Aviation Minor Software Engineering and App Development Minor It is an exciting time to study engineering and to be a part of a Christian college of engineering that intentionally combines solid technical training with people skills in a Christian context and biblical worldview. Let me invite you to contact me directly if you would like to know more about the CBU College of Engineering and how you might","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nintentionally combines solid technical training with people skills in a Christian context and biblical worldview. Let me invite you to contact me directly if you would like to know more about the CBU College of Engineering and how you might be able to become involved with us. Philip Van Haaster, DBA Dean of the Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering Why Study at the Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering? It is an exciting time to study engineering and to be a part of a Christian college of engineering that intentionally combines solid technical training with people skills in a Christian context and biblical worldview. Besides traditional vocational destinations, people trained as engineers go on to become lawyers, doctors, K-12 teachers, university professors, business entrepreneurs, salesperson, pastors and missionaries. The number one undergraduate background for Fortune 500 companies by discipline is engineering. Engineers have changed the course of world history and been prominent in missions efforts. The Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering at California Baptist University is a top Christian engineering college in California and is growing rapidly in terms of both students and facilities. In our first year, we started work on new labs and classroom space and have received numerous gifts and grants including a grant from","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nChristian engineering college in California and is growing rapidly in terms of both students and facilities. In our first year, we started work on new labs and classroom space and have received numerous gifts and grants including a grant from the National Science Foundation. In addition to our six areas of study, students and faculty are focusing their research efforts into the following areas: green technologies, rehabilitation technologies and technologies designed to improve life in developing parts of the world. Let me invite you to contact me directly if you would like to know more about the CBU College of Engineering and how you might be able to become involved with us. Distinctives: A caring, Christ-centered, learning community. Both \"excellent\" and \"average\" students will have opportunities for growth. Hands-on, team-oriented design projects all four years. Required internships within an industry or with a nonprofit organization. The mathematics required for engineering is taught just in time, in context and with","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\n\"excellent\" and \"average\" students will have opportunities for growth. Hands-on, team-oriented design projects all four years. Required internships within an industry or with a nonprofit organization. The mathematics required for engineering is taught just in time, in context and with hands-on labs. Training in project management and an exposure to the business side of engineering. Emphasis on both soft and hard engineering skill sets. All students will have a cross-cultural experience. All students are highly recommended to develop and maintain an electronic portfolio. Here for Your Purpose Schedule a Visit Places People Expand item The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Expand item The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Expand item The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Expand item CBU's Flight Operations Center is a 9,000 square-foot facility located at the Riverside Municipal Airport, containing classrooms, a flight planning room, flight simulators, full kitchen, student lounge, and maintenance and dispatch offices. Additionally, CBU has a brand-new fleet of Piper aircraft, allowing us to provide high-quality training","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nsquare-foot facility located at the Riverside Municipal Airport, containing classrooms, a flight planning room, flight simulators, full kitchen, student lounge, and maintenance and dispatch offices. Additionally, CBU has a brand-new fleet of Piper aircraft, allowing us to provide high-quality training in the most advanced glass-cockpit general aviation aircraft. Expand item CBU's Flight Operations Center is a 9,000 square-foot facility located at the Riverside Municipal Airport, containing classrooms, a flight planning room, flight simulators, full kitchen, student lounge, and maintenance and dispatch offices. Additionally, CBU has a brand-new fleet of Piper aircraft, allowing us to provide high-quality training in the most advanced glass-cockpit general aviation aircraft. Expand item CBU's Flight Operations Center is a 9,000 square-foot facility located at the Riverside Municipal Airport, containing classrooms, a flight planning room, flight simulators, full kitchen, student lounge, and maintenance and dispatch offices. Additionally, CBU has a brand-new fleet of Piper aircraft, allowing us to provide high-quality training in the most advanced glass-cockpit general aviation aircraft. Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item California Baptist University (CBU) is accredited by the Western Association of Schools and Colleges Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC). Regional accreditation by WSCUC means you can be confident that your CBU degree is recognized and respected","https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/\n\nitem Expand item California Baptist University (CBU) is accredited by the Western Association of Schools and Colleges Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC). Regional accreditation by WSCUC means you can be confident that your CBU degree is recognized and respected by schools and companies across the country. Study with the Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering at California Baptist University Join CBU's College of Engineering in engaging the world with the appropriate use of technology. Learn more about the College of Engineering and its programs, and find community here at CBU! Request More Information Apply for Admission ©","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/\n\nComputer Science, BCS Home > Academics > Programs > Computer Science, BCS Toggle video Computer Science, BCS Students who major in computer science will learn not just how to code but how to think. Request Info Program Intro While programming and design are two key aspects of computer science, the discipline also focuses on the theoretical, mathematical and algorithmic aspects of computation. Computer scientists are, along with software engineers, among the most in-demand professions in all fields of technology, and that’s only expected to grow. 114 Units *114-119 Units View All Courses 3 Concentrations 100 % Christian Faculty and Staff Why Study Computer Science at CBU? Gain real-world experience Students are trained in more advanced computing techniques, able to design and write computer programs in today’s programming languages and are thoroughly equipped to enter the mathematical sectors of the industry to fill roles that span both hardware and software development. Learn from a Christian worldview A unique aspect of the program is that students reflect on computer science from a Christian worldview, considering how to use code to solve global problems and promote good. Have a cross-cultural experience All engineering students are required to have a cross-cultural experience. This can be achieved several ways including participating in an International or Engineering","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/\n\nworldview, considering how to use code to solve global problems and promote good. Have a cross-cultural experience All engineering students are required to have a cross-cultural experience. This can be achieved several ways including participating in an International or Engineering Service Project (ISP or ESP), choosing a senior capstone project with cross-cultural emphasis, taking an approved intercultural studies course, or going on an approved study abroad. What You'll Learn CBU computer science graduates will have an ability to: Analyze a complex computing problem and to apply principles of computing and other relevant disciplines to identify solutions. Design, implement, and evaluate a computing-based solution to meet a given set of computing requirements in the context of the program’s discipline. Communicate effectively in a variety of professional contexts. Recognize professional responsibilities and make informed judgments in computing practice based on legal and ethical principles. Function effectively as a member or leader of a team engaged in activities appropriate to the program’s discipline. Apply computer science theory and software development fundamentals to produce computing-based solutions. Articulate a Christian worldview on personal, professional, technical, and societal issues. Develop attributes of leadership in an innovation-driven environment. View All Learning Outcomes Program Details Interesting Courses EGR 329: Computer Architecture Introduces students to the organization and architecture of computer systems, beginning","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/\n\nChristian worldview on personal, professional, technical, and societal issues. Develop attributes of leadership in an innovation-driven environment. View All Learning Outcomes Program Details Interesting Courses EGR 329: Computer Architecture Introduces students to the organization and architecture of computer systems, beginning with the standard von Neumann model and then moving forward to more recent architectural concepts. CSC 323: Cloud Computer and Application Development Investigation of modern cloud computing platforms and the practice of creating scalable software solutions that utilize essential cloud computing products. EGR 227: Data Structures and Analysis Fundamental data structures for implementation and analysis. Techniques for solving problems by programming. Analysis for complexity and performance trade-offs. View All Courses Concentrations Cybersecurity Concentration General Concentration Machine Learning and AI Concentration Accreditation CBU's bachelor degree program in computer science (BCS) has been accredited by the Computing Accreditation Commission of ABET, https://www.abet.org , under the General Criteria and the Computer Science and Similarly Named Computing","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/\n\nGeneral Concentration Machine Learning and AI Concentration Accreditation CBU's bachelor degree program in computer science (BCS) has been accredited by the Computing Accreditation Commission of ABET, https://www.abet.org , under the General Criteria and the Computer Science and Similarly Named Computing Programs Program Criteria. Meet Some of Our Faculty Dan Grissom, PhD Professor of Computer Science Department Chair for Computing, Software, and Data Sciences Chief Architect Office Phone: 951-552-8852 E-mail: dgrissom@calbaptist.edu Office Location: TEGR 331 Mark Kim, PhD Associate Professor of Computer and Data Sciences Director, CBU Ai and Machine Learning Lab (CAiMLL) Office Phone: 951-552-8786 E-mail: mkim@calbaptist.edu Office Location: College of Engineering Building, TEGR 332 I chose this major because I enjoy coding. I hope to aid others who don't have as many opportunities as me. I enjoy all of the professors as they have helped me become a better college student. Tristan Nguyen '22 I’ve always enjoyed logic-based puzzles so I find programming to be a lot of fun. Also, I’m interested in AI, OS and game development. I haven’t had a bad teacher yet and they’re always willing to help. Classes aren’t overfilled. Andrew Cate '21 Learn in a State-of-the-Art Engineering Building The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/\n\nyet and they’re always willing to help. Classes aren’t overfilled. Andrew Cate '21 Learn in a State-of-the-Art Engineering Building The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Request Info Exterior Interior Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Career Possibilities Computer science majors can apply their field of study to a variety of careers in the industry. Some careers listed below may require further education. Full-Stack Developer Front-End Developer Back-End Developer App Developer Software Developer Game Developer Systems Architect Technology Writer Data Scientist Related Programs Electrical and Computer Engineering, BSECE Mechanical Engineering, BSME Chemical Engineering, BSChE Request More Information Next Steps Join our community. At CBU, you’ll be challenged to become an individual whose skills, integrity, and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish you in the world. Request Information Visit Campus How to Apply ©","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor computer science\n\n/academics/programs/minor-computer-science.php Computer Science Minor With a minor in computer science, you can combine your major with the knowledge of computer science and have a dynamic combination, making you more competitive in the job market. Bachelors College of Engineering TEST","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor computer engineering\n\n/academics/programs/minor-computer-engineering.php Computer Engineering Minor The computer engineering minor from CBU allows students to gain hands-on experience building and designing computer hardware and software. Bachelors College of Engineering On Campus","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nComputer Science, MSCS Request Info Apply Now Home > Academics > Programs > Computer Science, MSCS Computer Science, MSCS The Master of Science in Computer Science program at California Baptist University is meticulously crafted to provide students with an in-depth and advanced understanding of computer science, enabling them to tackle complex problems and innovate in the field. Request Info Program Intro The Master of Science in Computer Science (MSCS) at California Baptist University offers an innovative curriculum designed to equip students with advanced skills to tackle the challenges of modern technology. With core courses in software engineering, advanced computer networks, advanced algorithms and advanced computer architecture, this program focuses on providing students with both the theoretical knowledge and practical experience needed to excel in today’s fast-paced tech landscape. Our program emphasizes analysis and problem-solving, preparing students to create impactful solutions in diverse fields like cybersecurity, machine learning, cloud computing and data science. Students have the flexibility to deepen their expertise across multiple breadth and depth areas such as cybersecurity, cloud and distributed computing, machine learning and big data. Through hands-on projects, research and exposure to real-world applications, students will build a strong foundation and become leaders in the field. The program also includes opportunities for students to attend colloquiums featuring industry experts and researchers working on disruptive developments, ensuring that","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nprojects, research and exposure to real-world applications, students will build a strong foundation and become leaders in the field. The program also includes opportunities for students to attend colloquiums featuring industry experts and researchers working on disruptive developments, ensuring that students are at the forefront of emerging technologies. Whether you’re interested in advancing your career or contributing to groundbreaking transformations, the MSCS program at CBU provides the tools, knowledge and support to help you succeed. 30 Units View All Courses 1- 2 Years* *1-year option available for qualified alumni. $ 720 +fees Cost Per Unit* *Based on current academic year. Subject to change. View Additional Cost of Attendance and Fees Why earn a Master of Science in Computer Science at CBU? Expand your foundational knowledge Core curriculum includes four advanced courses designed to expand students' foundational knowledge and skills in software engineering, advanced computer networks, advanced algorithms, and advanced computer architecture, ensuring students gain mastery over critical computer science concepts. Focus on practical experience The program emphasizes practical application through project-focused coursework. Students undertake a research-and-design capstone project to propose and implement a real-world solution to a significant modern challenge. Our curriculum is designed to stay current with industry standards and practices, ensuring graduates are equipped with the latest","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nthrough project-focused coursework. Students undertake a research-and-design capstone project to propose and implement a real-world solution to a significant modern challenge. Our curriculum is designed to stay current with industry standards and practices, ensuring graduates are equipped with the latest skills and knowledge required by employers. Take electives to suit your interests The MSCS program offers a diverse selection of electives, allowing students to either specialize in a specific sub-field or gain a broader perspective across multiple areas. Students can choose courses from the following key CS sub-fields: cybersecurity; cloud, distributed and parallel computing; machine learning and data mining; statistics and data science. Learn from a Christian worldview A unique aspect of the program is that students apply computer science solutions from a Christian worldview, positively resolving community challenges with honorable coding and algorithms. To ensure well-rounded professional development, students partake in the Computer Science Colloquium, engaging with contemporary issues, ethical considerations, and emerging trends in computer science. What You'll Learn CBU's Master of Science in Computer Science graduates will have an ability to: Analyze a complex computing problem and to apply principles of computing and other relevant disciplines to identify solutions. Design, implement, and evaluate a computing-based solution to meet a given set of","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nin Computer Science graduates will have an ability to: Analyze a complex computing problem and to apply principles of computing and other relevant disciplines to identify solutions. Design, implement, and evaluate a computing-based solution to meet a given set of computing requirements in the context of the program's discipline. Communicate effectively in a variety of professional contexts. Recognize professional responsibilities and make informed judgments in computing practice based on legal and ethical principles. Function effectively as a member or leader of a team engaged in activities appropriate to the program's discipline. Apply computer science theory and software development fundamentals to produce computing-based solutions. View All Learning Outcomes Program Details Interesting Courses CSC 433: Cyber Security Incident Response and Recovery This course provides an in-depth look to define the nature and scope of cyber security incident handling services, including intrusion/incident detection, damage control, service continuity, forensic analysis, service/data restoration, and incident reporting. CSC 434: Secure System Principles This course provides an advanced course that focuses on key principles of a constructive approach to secure systems. A brief review of operating systems and computer architecture is provided. CSC 501: Software Engineering This course presents modern software engineering techniques and examines the software life cycle, including software specification, design, implementation, testing, and","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nconstructive approach to secure systems. A brief review of operating systems and computer architecture is provided. CSC 501: Software Engineering This course presents modern software engineering techniques and examines the software life cycle, including software specification, design, implementation, testing, and maintenance. View All Courses Meet Some of Our Faculty Dan Grissom, PhD Professor of Computer Science Department Chair for Computing, Software, and Data Sciences Chief Architect Office Phone: 951-552-8852 E-mail: dgrissom@calbaptist.edu Office Location: TEGR 331 Esther Lee, Ph.D. Professor of Statistics Office Phone: 951-343-4942 E-mail: elee@calbaptist.edu Office Location: James 479 FAQs Are there concentrations offered in the Master of Computer Science? Although we don’t have concentrations, students have the option to pick 4 depth areas that they are most interested in. What are the program's entry dates and application deadline? The Master of Computer Science program admits students in the Fall and Spring. How long does the admission process take? Once an application file and/or interview is complete, applicants will typically be notified of a decision within 3–5 business days. View All FAQs How to Apply As you make decisions about graduate school, keep in mind the full social, spiritual and educational experience awaiting you at CBU. Here, you will be supported by professors, staff and fellow students who will help you discover your strengths and challenge","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nyou make decisions about graduate school, keep in mind the full social, spiritual and educational experience awaiting you at CBU. Here, you will be supported by professors, staff and fellow students who will help you discover your strengths and challenge you to do more than you ever thought you could. Let us equip you with the education, training and opportunities you'll need to start making a unique difference doing what you love. Application Deadlines Fall Spring Priority May 15 November 1 Standard August 1 December 1 Read Application Requirements Learn in a State-of-the-Art Engineering Building The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Request Info Exterior Interior Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Career Possibilities Master's level computer science graduates can apply their field of study to a variety","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/\n\nand labs. Request Info Exterior Interior Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Career Possibilities Master's level computer science graduates can apply their field of study to a variety of careers in the industry. Some careers listed below may require further education. Software Architect Data Scientist Cybersecurity Analyst Full-Stack Developer Front-End Developer Back-End Developer App Developer Software Developer Game Developer Systems Architect Technology Writer Related Programs Information Technology Management, MS Information Technology Management, MS (Online) Computer Science, BS Request More Information Next Steps Join our community. At CBU, you’ll be challenged to become an individual whose skills, integrity, and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish you in the world. Request Information Attend an Info Session How to Apply ©","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor data sciences\n\n/academics/programs/minor-data-sciences.php Data Science Minor The data science minor from CBU teaches students how to work with data and make meaningful, data-driven decisions. Bachelors College of Engineering On Campus","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\nData Science Request Info Apply Now Home > Academics > Programs > Data Science, BS Data Science, BS California Baptist University's data science major will prepare students to analyze large sets of data in any industry to help make informed, data-centric decisions. Request Info A Premier Data Science Program in Southern California A premier data science program in Southern California, CBU's data science major will allow students to learn the foundations in computing, mathematics and statistics to become true data scientists. Students will not only \"push buttons\" to generate basic models but be able to design and program their own unique statistical models or advance state-of-the-art algorithms to solve real-world problems. 104 Units *104-109 units View All Courses 36 % Expected Rate of Growth The projected percent change in employment for data scientists from 2023 to 2033. Source: US Bureau of Labor Statistics 100 % Christian Faculty and Staff Why Study Data Science at CBU? Cover the Entire Data Science Lifecycle California Baptist University's data science program covers the entire data science lifecycle, from data acquisition to visualization, and teaches students how to apply data science techniques to solve real-world problems. The program combines a strong computational core with foundations in relevant mathematics and statistics, enabling students to develop and program their own statistical models or advance state-of-the-art","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\nand teaches students how to apply data science techniques to solve real-world problems. The program combines a strong computational core with foundations in relevant mathematics and statistics, enabling students to develop and program their own statistical models or advance state-of-the-art algorithms. Study Cutting-Edge Data Tools and Modern Methods Students will learn cutting-edge tools, models and theory in modern areas of machine learning, such as deep learning, data mining, natural language processing, and more. With both high- and low-level understandings of model development and usage in place, students are taught how to visualize their data using modern toolsets and strategies. Furthermore, CBU's data science program offers unique coursework developing geospatial models and visualization strategies in the emerging field of geospatial information systems (GIS). Learn Through Experience Students leverage data science coursework to identify a problem and create a significant group-based junior design \"data science product (solution),\" which is ideated, designed, planned and built in the context of a tech startup company. During their senior year, students embark on a year-long, group-based senior capstone project where they work with a client to gather requirements, plan and build a significant software and/or statistical product to solve a customer problem. Emphasis on Ethics CBU's data science program incorporates ethical and proper usage of data across the","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\nproject where they work with a client to gather requirements, plan and build a significant software and/or statistical product to solve a customer problem. Emphasis on Ethics CBU's data science program incorporates ethical and proper usage of data across the curriculum, and also offers unique and in-depth coursework in geospatial information systems (GIS) and data product development. While developing and understanding models is fundamental to the data science lifecycle, real-world solutions must ensure that the data being input into these models is fair, representative and un-biased. In addition to specific coursework in data wrangling, instruction on the ethical and proper usage of data is incorporated across curriculum where machine learning and statistical models are studied. What You'll Learn Students graduating with a degree in data science will learn to: Analyze a complex computing problem and to apply principles of computing and other relevant disciplines to identify solutions. Design, implement, and evaluate a computing-based solution to meet a given set of computing requirements in the context of the program's discipline. Communicate effectively in a variety of professional contexts. Recognize professional responsibilities and make informed judgments in computing practice based on legal and ethical principles. Function effectively as a member or leader of a team engaged in activities appropriate","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\nprogram's discipline. Communicate effectively in a variety of professional contexts. Recognize professional responsibilities and make informed judgments in computing practice based on legal and ethical principles. Function effectively as a member or leader of a team engaged in activities appropriate to the program's discipline. Apply theory, techniques, and tools throughout the data science lifecycle and employ the resulting knowledge to satisfy stakeholders' needs. Program Details Interesting Courses CSC 422 : Machine and Deep Learning This course covers introductory machine learning topics including supervised and unsupervised learning, linear and logistic regression, support vector machines, neural networks (MLPs, CNNs, RNNs, GANs) and more. CSC 442 : Intermediate GIS (Geographic Information Systems) Elevate organizational strategic thinking by addressing intricate challenges through the application of established data and cutting-edge Geographic Information System (GIS) solutions. CSC 423 : Data Mining The course commences with an examination of the knowledge discovery process. In particular, the introduction equips students with the strategic thinking skills essential to focus on cutting edge data mining techniques that can be applied in a wide variety of settings,","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\ncourse commences with an examination of the knowledge discovery process. In particular, the introduction equips students with the strategic thinking skills essential to focus on cutting edge data mining techniques that can be applied in a wide variety of settings, e.g., business, engineering, health care, science, etc. View All Courses Meet Some of Our Faculty Anthony Corso, Ph.D. Professor of Computer Information Systems Office Phone: 951-343-4794 E-mail: acorso@calbaptist.edu Office Location: College of Engineering Building, TEGR 330 Esther Lee, Ph.D. Professor of Statistics Office Phone: 951-343-4942 E-mail: elee@calbaptist.edu Office Location: James 479 Career Possibilities The data science program at CBU prepares students to fill roles as leaders, decision makers, solutions developers and consultants in almost any organization that uses data to solve real-world problems. Students will be equipped with the fundamental understandings of computing systems and statistical modeling needed to enter graduate school or advance the state of the art in data science. (Note that some careers listed below may require further education.) Data Scientist Data Engineer Data Analyst Machine Learning Engineer Database Administrator Data Architect Research Statistician Educator Related Programs Statistics and Data Analytics, BA Mathematics, BS Sports Analytics, BS Request More Information Next Steps Join our community. At CBU, you’ll be challenged to become an individual whose skills,","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/\n\nLearning Engineer Database Administrator Data Architect Research Statistician Educator Related Programs Statistics and Data Analytics, BA Mathematics, BS Sports Analytics, BS Request More Information Next Steps Join our community. At CBU, you’ll be challenged to become an individual whose skills, integrity, and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish you in the world. Request Information Visit Campus How to Apply ©","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nElectrical and Computer Engineering, BSECE Home > Academics > Programs > Electrical and Computer Engineering, BSECE Toggle video Electrical and Computer Engineering, BSECE From supercomputers and robotics to voice recognition and renewable energy, electrical and computer engineers are improving the quality of life everywhere in the world. Request Info Program Intro CBU’s electrical and computer engineering program trains students to become a problem solver and innovator with the technologies of the 21st century. As this is a profession in great demand, our graduates have found employment at leading engineering companies such as Intel, Apple and Southern California Edison. 112 Units *112-117 Units View All Courses 3 Years to Complete CBU's engineering accelerated program will allow students to earn their Bachelor of Science in Electrical and Computer Engineering in as little as 3 years. 100 % Christian Faculty and Staff Why Study Electrical and Computer Engineering at CBU? Work in the industry and gain real-world experience One of the most important parts of students’ college training will be learning in a real engineering job. Students have a special course to prepare them for this first engineering job. Their official internship will be the summer of their junior year,","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nthe most important parts of students’ college training will be learning in a real engineering job. Students have a special course to prepare them for this first engineering job. Their official internship will be the summer of their junior year, although other opportunities may occur for them to work in the industry. Learn how to manage projects Industrial representatives continue to emphasize that they wish new engineers had more project management experience. Engineering students in their junior year work with and co-mentor business students. Our goal is that eventually all students in our program will develop one of their own creative ideas and go through the process to receive a provisional patent. Have a cross-cultural experience All engineering students are required to have a cross-cultural experience. This can be achieved several ways including participating in an International or Engineering Service Project (ISP or ESP), choosing a senior capstone project with cross-cultural emphasis, taking an approved intercultural studies course, or going on an approved study abroad. Complete in 3 years CBU offers an engineering accelerated program that will allow you to earn your Bachelor","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nESP), choosing a senior capstone project with cross-cultural emphasis, taking an approved intercultural studies course, or going on an approved study abroad. Complete in 3 years CBU offers an engineering accelerated program that will allow you to earn your Bachelor of Science in Electrical and Computer Engineering in as little as 3 years. What You'll Learn CBU electrical and computer engineering graduates are expected to acquire: An ability to identify, formulate, and solve complex engineering problems by applying principles of engineering, science, and mathematics. A n ability to apply engineering design to produce solutions that meet specified needs with consideration of public health, safety, and welfare, as well as global, cultural, social, environmental, and economic factors. A n ability to communicate effectively with a range of audiences. A n ability to recognize ethical and professional responsibilities in engineering situations and make informed judgments, which must consider the impact of engineering solutions in global, economic, environmental, and societal contexts. A n ability to function effectively on a team whose members together provide leadership, create a collaborative and inclusive environment, establish goals, plan tasks, and meet objectives. A n ability to develop and conduct appropriate experimentation, analyze and interpret data, and use engineering judgment to draw conclusions. A n ability to acquire and apply new knowledge as needed, using appropriate learning","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\ngoals, plan tasks, and meet objectives. A n ability to develop and conduct appropriate experimentation, analyze and interpret data, and use engineering judgment to draw conclusions. A n ability to acquire and apply new knowledge as needed, using appropriate learning strategies. A n ability to articulate a Christian worldview on personal, professional, technical, and societal issues. A n ability to develop attributes of leadership in an innovation-driven environment. View All Learning Outcomes Program Details Interesting Courses EGR 302: Engineering Design and Documentation Team design of industrial or self-designed projects. EGR 321: Computer Organization Study of organization and structuring of the major hardware and software components of computers. EGR 333: Electronics I Study of electronic devices and basic circuit configurations. View All Courses Accelerated 3-Year Electrical and Computer Engineering Program The Gordon and Jill Bourns College of Engineering at California Baptist University is proud to offer an accelerated program that will allow you to earn your Bachelor of Science degree in as little as 3 years! Who should look into the 3-year electrical and computer engineering program? High school seniors accepted to CBU engineering who have taken AP Calculus AB exam","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nallow you to earn your Bachelor of Science degree in as little as 3 years! Who should look into the 3-year electrical and computer engineering program? High school seniors accepted to CBU engineering who have taken AP Calculus AB exam with a score of 4 or above (or AP Calculus BC with a score of 3 or above) and two other AP subjects valid for degree requirements in the College of Engineering at CBU. STEM-minded, highly-motived, and hard-working high school sophomore and junior students who are planning to pursue an engineering degree. What are the advantages of the 3-year electrical and computer engineering program? Saving tens of thousands of dollars on tuition and other expenses. Giving yourself a jump-start on your professional career. Allowing you to advance earlier into a post-graduate degree. Increasing your lifetime income by starting to work one year earlier. How does it work? Inform your CBU admission counselor that you would like to pursue a 3-year BS degree in electrical and computer engineering . Send your AP or CLEP scores to CBU. Develop an academic plan to be reviewed and approved by your department chair before your first week of class at CBU. Work with your faculty academic advisor who will monitor your progress and help you","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nor CLEP scores to CBU. Develop an academic plan to be reviewed and approved by your department chair before your first week of class at CBU. Work with your faculty academic advisor who will monitor your progress and help you resolve schedule conflicts throughout the next three years. Expect to t ake a full load of 17-18 units of courses each semester. You will possibly need to take courses during the summer after your first and/ or third year(s). A required 200-hour internship will mostly likely take place during the summer after your 2 nd year. Therefore students on the 3-year plan might not be able to use that summer to take courses . Request More Info Accreditation CBU's bachelor degree program in electrical and computer engineering, BSECE has been accredited by the Engineering Accreditation Commission of ABET, https://www.abet.org , under the General Criteria and the Electrical, Computer, Communications, Telecommunication(s) and Similarly Named Engineering Programs Program Criteria. Meet Some of Our Faculty John Butler, Ph.D. Associate Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering Chair, Electrical & Computer Engineering Office Phone: 951-552-8363 E-mail: jbutler@calbaptist.edu Office Location: College of Engineering Building, TEGR 325 As an engineer at the world’s largest aerospace company, my work helps shape the course","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nAssociate Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering Chair, Electrical & Computer Engineering Office Phone: 951-552-8363 E-mail: jbutler@calbaptist.edu Office Location: College of Engineering Building, TEGR 325 As an engineer at the world’s largest aerospace company, my work helps shape the course of history and improves the lives and safety of so many people here in the U.S. and around the world. Patrick Nsengiyumva '12 Digital Design and Radiation Effects Engineer, The Boeing Company Read Patrick's Story I chose electrical and computer engineering because I wanted to become more familiar with power and control systems since they are very relevant in progressing today's technology. I also feel like my major has prepared me to be able to work in other STEM-related fields. The classes I take make me feel like I am broadening my perspectives and overcoming my limits. Claire Pamintuan '19 I would like to go into the solar power engineering field. The lab courses introduce certain products that all ECE students should become familiar with. Like many students before me, I have struggled to get where I am. Frankly, I am grateful for the group that makes up the ECE student body because I know that my experience at some other","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nshould become familiar with. Like many students before me, I have struggled to get where I am. Frankly, I am grateful for the group that makes up the ECE student body because I know that my experience at some other school would be way more negative than that of my experience here at CBU. Roberto Sanchez '19 I chose ECE since I've always been fascinated with how circuits and electricity work, and I wanted to learn more about them. My professors are very engaging and encourage us to ask questions often. Having classes of around 15–25 students makes it easy to get to know the professor and the other students well. Michael Dubisz '20 CBU’s College of Engineering is the only school that I am aware of that it would have been possible to complete an engineering degree in 3 years. Not only was it possible to do this but ECE faculty helped make my goal a reality. Armando Chacon '20 Distribution Engineer 1, Southern California Edison Read Armando's Story Student Opportunities: SAE Club Students in CBU's SAE Car Club compete annually in competitions, gaining experience with SAE car design, a prerequisite for employment with SpaceX, and other engineering companies. In June 2021, at the Las Vegas competition, the Lancers placed fourth overall,","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/\n\nClub Students in CBU's SAE Car Club compete annually in competitions, gaining experience with SAE car design, a prerequisite for employment with SpaceX, and other engineering companies. In June 2021, at the Las Vegas competition, the Lancers placed fourth overall, ahead of all other California schools. Learn More Learn in a State-of-the-Art Engineering Building The Dennis and Carol Troesh Engineering Building at California Baptist University features numerous collaborative workspaces for students in addition to state-of-the-art classrooms and labs. Request Info Exterior Interior Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Expand item Career Possibilities Electrical and computer engineering majors can apply their field of study to a variety of careers in the engineering industry. Some careers listed below may require further education. Aerospace Engineer Automotive Engineer Computer Engineer Radio-Frequency Engineer Microelectronics IC Designer Digital Signal Processing Specialist Power Electronics Specialist Robotics Engineer Utilities and Power Systems Engineer Related Programs Chemical Engineering, BSChE Mechanical Engineering, BSME Computer Science, BS Request More Information Next Steps Join our community. At CBU, you’ll be challenged to become an individual whose skills, integrity, and sense of purpose glorify God and distinguish you in the world. Request Information Visit Campus How to Apply ©","https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor software engineering and app development\n\n/academics/programs/minor-software-engineering-and-app-development.php Software Engineering and App Development Minor Have a major impact on the world by minoring in software engineering. A minor in software engineering can dramatically expand your career opportunities. Bachelors College of Engineering On Campus Software Engineering Minor, Systems Analyst, Software Engineer, Database Administrator, Project Manager, Computer Game Developer, Software Engineering and App Development Minor","Authorizations and Variances\n\nAuthorizations and Variances The two most common ways in which the CSDS department chair serves students is through authorizations and variances. Variance: Allows a student to count another (CBU or non-CBU) course toward a CBU degree requirement (e.g., course) Authorization: Allows a student to enroll into a course in which they do not meet the pre-requisites Variances A variance allows a student to count another (CBU or non-CBU) course toward a CBU degree requirement (e.g., course). Common scenarios in which students may request a variance include, but are not limited to: Student has an equivalent course they took at another school in which the credit transferred to CBU, but there is no known/automatic agreement giving you credit for that course in your degree requirements Ex: Student took an intro programming course at \"Local Valley College\", for which there is no existing transfer agreement, that covers much or all of the same materials we cover in EGR 121; you submit the variance request (including syllabus) and we decide to give you credit for EGR 121. Student has an equivalent or similar course they took at CBU that they want considered as a suitable alternative Ex: Student took a proofs class in the Math department and wants to request it count for EGR 225 Discrete Structures course, which is very similar","Authorizations and Variances\n\nsimilar course they took at CBU that they want considered as a suitable alternative Ex: Student took a proofs class in the Math department and wants to request it count for EGR 225 Discrete Structures course, which is very similar in content and objectives. Formal Variance Process and Forms 8/31/26, 1:14 PM Authorizations and Variances https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Authorizations-and-Variances.aspx 1/2 The following steps must ALL be completed before a decision can be made on your variance request: 1. Check that the course will not automatically transfer using transferology.com (instructions here) a. If there is already an agreement, you do not need to file a variance request...it will automatically count 2. Complete the CSDS Variance Request Form, making sure to upload your syllabus and/or supporting documents a. This form helps provide the CSDS chair with the specific information needed to decide on a request 3. Complete the CBU Variance Request Form a. This form triggers the formal process with the register, which will come back to the CSDS department chair for approval (hence, why the above form is required to ensure the chair has the info needed) Authorizations An authorization allows a student to enroll into a course in which they do not meet the pre-requisites. Common scenarios in which students may request a variance include, but are not limited to:","Authorizations and Variances\n\nthe chair has the info needed) Authorizations An authorization allows a student to enroll into a course in which they do not meet the pre-requisites. Common scenarios in which students may request a variance include, but are not limited to: Student is waiting for a variance request to be formally approved, but needs to enroll in a course now to secure a seat in the course Student has time conflict with two courses that meet at the same time and the two instructors and department chair have agreed on an acceptable solution NOTE: This is generally only approved in your last year if it is determined that you need both courses to graduate, there is no other solution and you have demonstrated academic success in your previous years at CBU Student cannot enroll into a course because of a capacity limit NOTE: The department chair can often add individuals, so long as the room can fit the increased capacity and the instructor feels the increased capacity will not detract from the course objectives Formal Authorization Process and Forms All authorization requests should be submitted via the following form and will not be accepted or considered via e-mail:","Authorizations and Variances\n\nincreased capacity and the instructor feels the increased capacity will not detract from the course objectives Formal Authorization Process and Forms All authorization requests should be submitted via the following form and will not be accepted or considered via e-mail: NOTE: You do NOT need to e-mail the department chair before or after your request...the system will automatically alert the chair when a new request is submitted. 1. CSDS Authorization Request Form 8/31/26, 1:14 PM Authorizations and Variances https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Authorizations-and-Variances.aspx 2/2","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nComputer Science Course Transfer Guide California Baptist University (CBU) CS Courses and Potential Transfer Details Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions CSC312 Algorithms 3 Units EGR227 Algorithms are the heart of any computer software; they define the procedure for accomplishing a data-intensive task.  This  course  establishes  terms  and  methods  for talking about algorithms, examines some of the basic algorithms for sorting, searching and other fundamental tasks,  and  explores  more  advanced  algorithms  in bioinformatics and other fields. Advanced Algorithms (e.g., Dynamic Programming, Advanced Search/Sort, Proofs of Correction, Parallel Programming, Matrix Multiplication), Time/Space Complexity, Programming Experience, Algorithm Design CSC313 Graphics and Visual Computing 3 Units EGR227 This course explores issues related to the production and the analysis of imagery and visual representations. Computer graphics are introduced from basic concepts through rendering and animation. Visualization of data is presented. Computer Vision and Image Processing is presented both mathematically and practically with an emphasis on creating image manipulation programs in a high-level language. Applied 3D Graphics in a Modern Engine (e.g., Unity, Unreal), Technical Game Design, Rendering, 2D Simulation, 3D Simulation and Physics Incorporation (optional), Parallel Programming (optional) CSC321 Web Application Development 3 Units EGR222 The  design  and  development  of  data  driven  web applications. The integration and exploitation of HTML,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\n(e.g., Unity, Unreal), Technical Game Design, Rendering, 2D Simulation, 3D Simulation and Physics Incorporation (optional), Parallel Programming (optional) CSC321 Web Application Development 3 Units EGR222 The design and development of data driven web applications. The integration and exploitation of HTML, JavaScript,  server-side  programming  languages  and database technology. Web-App Development (beyond HTML/ CSS), Javascript or Python Web Front- End, Database Integration (e.g., SQL, MongoDB) CSC322 Mobile Application Development 3 Units EGR222 Investigation of mobile operating systems and associated software  development  environments.  Consideration  of unique constraints and techniques for creating software designed for mobile devices. Design and development of several  mobile  applications  targeted  at  the  primary mobile operating systems. Mobile App Creation, Modern Cross- Platform Languages (e.g., Flutter, React Native) or Modern Native Languages (e.g., Kotlin/Java for Android, Swift for iOS) CSC323 Cloud Comp and App Devel 3 Units EGR226 Investigation of modern cloud computing platforms and the practice of creating scalable software solutions that utilize essential cloud computing products like serverless computing,  serverless  key-value  NOSQL  databases, cloud storage, publisher/subscriber messaging, identity access and permissions management, machine learning (ML) integration, etc. Cloud Application Setup (GCP, Azure, or AWS), Serverless Computing, IAM Permissions 1 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions CSC415 TheoryComputationandCompilers 3 Units EGR225, EGR227","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\netc. Cloud Application Setup (GCP, Azure, or AWS), Serverless Computing, IAM Permissions 1 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions CSC415 TheoryComputationandCompilers 3 Units EGR225, EGR227 Introduction to concepts and considerations of modern compilers  and  programming  languages.  Language translation,  types  and  declaration,  and  abstraction mechanisms  are  studied.  Both  functional  and  object- oriented programming paradigms are explored through examination  of  several  programming  languages. Automata,  languages  and  grammar,  language processing, computability and complexity theory will be examined  in  detail.  Important  topics  will  be  explored using  a  combination  of  conceptual  work  and  coding exercises. Automata, Language/Grammar, Proofs by Induction, Kleene Algebra (optional), Minimal Compiler Construction CSC421 Artificial Intelligence 3 Units EGR227 Artificial Intelligence (AI) is concerned with the design and analysis of autonomous agents that perceive their environment and make rational decisions. This course introduces the basic definitions and issues in Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning. Students will create reasoning  systems  in  software  and  explore  their capabilities  in  dealing  with  new  knowledge.  Ethical issues  in  AI  will  be  presented  and  examined  from  a Christian perspective. Search-based Agents (e.g., BFS, DFS, A*, UCS), First-order or Propositional Logic (minor), Constraint Satisfaction Problems (minor) CSC422 Machine and Deep Learning 3 Units EGR121 or (EGR120, EGR305) or MAT353 or STA144 or STA310","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\npresented and examined from a Christian perspective. Search-based Agents (e.g., BFS, DFS, A*, UCS), First-order or Propositional Logic (minor), Constraint Satisfaction Problems (minor) CSC422 Machine and Deep Learning 3 Units EGR121 or (EGR120, EGR305) or MAT353 or STA144 or STA310 This course covers introductory machine learning topics including supervised and unsupervised learning, linear and logistic regression, support vector machines, neural networks  (MLPs,  CNNs,  RNNs,  GANs)  and  more. Coursework  includes  instruction  and  programming assignments in algorithmic implementations and high- level library usage. Students also apply machine learning techniques to a unique research project. Basic Neural Networks in Modern Platforms (e.g., TensorFlow, PyTorch, Keras, SciKit) CSC423 Data Mining 3 Units EGR121 or (EGR120, EGR305) or MAT353 or STA144 or STA310 The  course  commences  with  an  examination  of  the knowledge  discovery  process.  In  particular,  the introduction equips students with the strategic thinking skills  essential  to  focus  on  cutting  edge  data  mining techniques  that  can  be  applied  in  a  wide  variety  of settings,  e.g.,  business,  engineering,  health  care, science,  etc.  Traditional  topics  include  data  mining algorithms and implementation issues, advantages and disadvantages  of  data  mining,  and  examples  of knowledge  engineering.  Current  topics  such  as ubiquitous,  distributed,  and  spatiotemporal  geographic data mining will also be explored. This is a practical hands-on course that culminates in a real-world project implemented via open source tools.","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nof data mining, and examples of knowledge engineering. Current topics such as ubiquitous, distributed, and spatiotemporal geographic data mining will also be explored. This is a practical hands-on course that culminates in a real-world project implemented via open source tools. Data Mining, Data Processing, Predictive Modeling, Pattern Discovery, Decision Trees, Clustering, Association Rules, Knowledge Extraction, Data Preprocessing, Supervised Learning, Unsupervised Learning, Model Evaluation 2 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions CSC424 Natural Language Processing 3 Units EGR121 or (EGR120, EGR305) or MAT353 or STA144 or STA310 Natural language is ubiquitous, e.g., humans speak and write  to  communicate,  to  transfer  information,  and  to document  knowledge.  Natural  Language  Processing (NLP) is an integral component in countless information systems  requiring  advanced  manipulation  of  natural language. In this class, students will be introduced to NLP starting with the concept of understanding words in context and the need for natural language processing in the business environment. The discussion continues with a  detailed  study  of  words  and  is  a  foundational framework supporting phonetics and speech synthesis. Subsequent topics include concepts of how words are grouped together to form unique grammatical units. The last part of the course, explores solving real-world NLP problems and deals with two key areas: corpus building,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nframework supporting phonetics and speech synthesis. Subsequent topics include concepts of how words are grouped together to form unique grammatical units. The last part of the course, explores solving real-world NLP problems and deals with two key areas: corpus building, feature  engineering,  and  application  development. Course material is presented via theory-based lectures, group  discussion,  and  practical  labs-a  culminating research project will be individually crafted. NLP Corpus Management, Fundamental NLP Theories, NLP Tools (e.g., NLTK, spaCy), Processing Models, Corpus Parsing, Text Classification, Sentiment Analysis, Question Answering, Information Extraction CSC431 InfoSecurityAndComptrForensics 3 Units EGR222 This  course  provides  an  introduction  to  the  field  of Security in computing. Topics include common security standards  and  policies,  cryptography  and  information security, access controls, attacks and countermeasures, and computer forensics. Ethics and Legal Frameworks, Attack and Defense Strategies in Software, Social Engineering Strategies and Defense CSC432 Network Traffic Analysis 3 Units CSC431 This  course  provides  an  in-depth  look  into  the fundamentals of packet-switched network traffic analysis at the network layer and above as applied to problems in traffic engineering, economics, security, etc. The course will explore the design and integration of analytic tools and techniques into the fabric of the network including: spatial  and  temporal  anomaly  detection,  origin- destination  matrix  estimation,  application  mix determination,  deep-packet  inspection,  fingerprinting, intrusion detection and insider threat mitigation. Finally,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nwill explore the design and integration of analytic tools and techniques into the fabric of the network including: spatial and temporal anomaly detection, origin- destination matrix estimation, application mix determination, deep-packet inspection, fingerprinting, intrusion detection and insider threat mitigation. Finally, the course covers active defense and offensive methods reliant on traffic analysis. Contains a lab component. Packet-switched network traffic analysis, Intrusion detection, Insider threat mitigation, Active defense, Offensive methods 3 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions CSC433 CyberSctyIncidentRspnAndRcvry 3 Units CSC431 This  course  provides  an  in-depth  look  to  define  the nature  and  scope  of  cyber  security  incident  handling services, including intrusion/incident detection, damage control, service continuity, forensic analysis, service/data restoration, and incident reporting. Material covers policy, planning, operations, and technology issues involved in related  cyber  incident  handling  plans;  i.e.,  Business Continuity,  Disaster  Recovery,  and  Continuity  of Operations. Specific incident types addressed include, natural  disasters,  denial  of  service,  malicious  code, malicious  misuse  of  hardware  and  firmware, unauthorized  access,  data  compromise  and inappropriate use, including insider attacks. Emphasis is given  to  the  detection  and  analysis  of  infiltration  and exfiltration  techniques  employed  during  cyber  attacks, thus enabling the incident handler to detect low noise attacks, and to deconstruct particularly insidious attacks. Contains a lab component.","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nincluding insider attacks. Emphasis is given to the detection and analysis of infiltration and exfiltration techniques employed during cyber attacks, thus enabling the incident handler to detect low noise attacks, and to deconstruct particularly insidious attacks. Contains a lab component. Cyber security incident handling, Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery, Forensic analysis and restoration CSC434 Secure System Principles 3 Units CSC431 This course provides an advanced course that focuses on key principles of a constructive approach to secure systems.  A  brief  review  of  operating  systems  and computer architecture is provided. Major topics include threat  characterization  and  subversion;  confinement; fundamental abstractions, principles, and mechanisms, such as reduced complexity, hierarchical relationships, least  privilege,  hardware  protection,  resource management and virtualization, software security, secure system composition, mutual suspicion, synchronization, covert  and  side-channel  analysis,  secure  metadata, secure  operational  states,  usability,  and  life  cycle assurance. Current developments will include advances in security hardware, components, and systems. This course has heavy reliance on software development and implementation. Secure System Composition, Threat Characterization and Subversion, Hardware Protection and Virtualization CST130 New Testament Survey 3 Units None listed A survey of the books of the New Testament and their contributions to the overall storyline of the Bible. Special attention  is  given  to  the  life  of  Jesus  and  the development  of  the  early  church  in  their  historical,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nNone listed A survey of the books of the New Testament and their contributions to the overall storyline of the Bible. Special attention is given to the life of Jesus and the development of the early church in their historical, cultural,  and  theological  settings.  Fulfills  general education requirement for non-Christian Studies majors. New Testament Survey, Life of Jesus, Early Church Development CST370 The Christian Faith 3 Units None listed An exploration of the biblical truths of Scripture, God, humanity,  sin,  Jesus,  the  Holy  Spirit,  salvation,  the Christian life, the church, and the life to come. Students will  examine  each  major  doctrine  and  understand  its place in the biblical story. Biblical truths, Christian life, Major doctrines 4 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions EGR101 Introduction to Engineering 3 Units None listed The course serves as an introduction to the exciting field and vocation of engineering and the value of engineering education.  This  course  includes  guest  lectures describing various engineering disciplines, team-based introductory  projects,  community  service,  and  weekly readings  that  focus  on  how  to  be  successful  as  an engineering student. The weekly readings include topics on  self-discovery,  goal  setting,  work-life  balance,  and understanding the teaching/learning process. A course emphasis includes Christian perspectives on purpose,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\ncommunity service, and weekly readings that focus on how to be successful as an engineering student. The weekly readings include topics on self-discovery, goal setting, work-life balance, and understanding the teaching/learning process. A course emphasis includes Christian perspectives on purpose, integrity, and service as they relate to the vocation of engineering.  This  is  the  first  course  required  of  all students considering engineering as a major. Note: This is a required course for Engineering, Computer Science, and Construction Management Majors. Introduction to engineering, Engineering disciplines, Team-based projects EGR102 Introduction Engineering Design 4 Units None listed Introduction  to  fundamental  techniques  used  in engineering design and analysis. Different models of the design process will be examined. A collaborative team oriented design project will be undertaken. Engineering Design and Analysis, Models of the Design Process, Team- Oriented Design Project EGR120 Intro Computer Programming Python 3 Units MAT115 or EGR181 Introduction  to  computer  science.  Covers  problem solving  methods  and  algorithm  development,  modern programming methodologies, and fundamentals of high- level block structured language using Python. Introductory-level programming (e.g., Java, C++, Python), Basic operators, Variables, Loops, Classes EGR121 Intro Computer Programming C++ 3 Units MAT115 or EGR181 Introduction  to  computer  science.  Covers  problem solving  methods  and  algorithm  development,  modern programming methodologies, and fundamentals of high- level block structured language using C++. Introductory-level Programming, Basic","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nVariables, Loops, Classes EGR121 Intro Computer Programming C++ 3 Units MAT115 or EGR181 Introduction to computer science. Covers problem solving methods and algorithm development, modern programming methodologies, and fundamentals of high- level block structured language using C++. Introductory-level Programming, Basic Operators, Variables, Loops, Classes, Programming Languages (e.g., Java, C++, or Python) EGR122 Visualization Languages I 3 Units None listed Engineering is a discipline which requires the effective communication  of  visual  information  as  part  of persuasion or education. Excel (beginning and advanced techniques),  and  a  CAD  program  will  be  covered  to assist  in  that  process  for  a  real  current  engineering problem of interest. For example this might include the utilization of solar power in Riverside County to address energy  consumption  concerns.  This  course  lays  the foundation for future courses which have elements of data and information presentation. Excel techniques, CAD programming, Data and information presentation 5 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions EGR182 Intro Math for Engineering App 3 Units EGR181 or MAT115 An  introduction  to  the  mathematical  foundations  of engineering. Topics include algebra, trigonometry, limits, geometry,  vectors,  complex  numbers,  sinusoids  and harmonic signals, systems of equations and matrices, derivatives,  and  integrals,  within  the  context  of  an engineering  application.  These  concepts  will  be","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nAn introduction to the mathematical foundations of engineering. Topics include algebra, trigonometry, limits, geometry, vectors, complex numbers, sinusoids and harmonic signals, systems of equations and matrices, derivatives, and integrals, within the context of an engineering application. These concepts will be reinforced through extensive examples of their use in the core engineering curriculum. Mathematical foundations of engineering, Algebra and trigonometry, Calculus and vectors EGR182L Intro Math for Engr App Lab 1 Units None listed This lab course will focus on engineering applications of the  math  topics  covered  in  ENGR1820  and  MATLAB programming  skills.  The  lab  sessions  include experiments involving math topics most heavily used in the core sophomore-level engineering courses, such as trigonometry, vectors, complex numbers, sinusoids and harmonic signals, and calculus. Matlab programming, Engineering math applications, Trigonometry, Calculus EGR222 Software Engineering 3 Units EGR121 or EGR120 Overview of the software development process. Includes requirements,  design,  construction,  and  testing  of software.  Software  project  planning.  Analysis, architecture, and design of software systems using UML. Evaluating  designs.  Implementing  designs  using appropriate data structures, frameworks, and APIs. Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction, Advanced Object Oriented Programming (OOP) EGR225 Discrete Structures I 3 Units None listed Introduces the foundations of discrete mathematics as they apply to computer science, focusing on providing a solid  theoretical  foundation  for  further  work.  Topics include  functions,  relations,  sets,  simple  proof","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nOriented Programming (OOP) EGR225 Discrete Structures I 3 Units None listed Introduces the foundations of discrete mathematics as they apply to computer science, focusing on providing a solid theoretical foundation for further work. Topics include functions, relations, sets, simple proof techniques, Boolean algebra, propositional logic, digital logic, elementary number theory, and the fundamentals of counting. Set theory, Proofs, Logic EGR226 Operating Systems and Networking 3 Units EGR222 Introduces  the  fundamentals  of  operating  systems including  processes,  memory,  scheduling,  input/output together with the basics of networking protocols. Operating system fundamentals, Networking protocols, Process and memory management EGR227 Data Structures and Analysis 3 Units EGR222 Fundamental  data  structures  for  implementation  and analysis.  Techniques  for  solving  problems  by programming. Analysis for complexity and performance trade-offs.  Topics  include  object  oriented  design, debugging, abstract data types (ADTs), recursion, big-O notation,  interfaces,  inheritance,  and  encapsulation, linked lists, stacks, queues, hash tables, heap, trees, searching, sorting. Time/Space Complexity, Stacks, Queues, Trees 6 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions EGR302 Engnrng Design and Documentation 3 Units None listed Team  design  of  industrial  or  self-designed  projects. Requires the design and development of a process or product with oral and written reports. Includes a review and analysis of professional papers. Team Design, Product Development,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nand Documentation 3 Units None listed Team design of industrial or self-designed projects. Requires the design and development of a process or product with oral and written reports. Includes a review and analysis of professional papers. Team Design, Product Development, Professional Paper Analysis EGR304 Leadership Cohort 1 Units None listed Preparation for a lifetime of leadership as an engineer. Small  group  discussion  format,  with  opportunities  for student facilitated discussions. Topics include: leadership in organizations, emotional intelligence, the psychology of small group dynamics and team performance, global perspectives  of  engineering.  Written  executive summaries as part of a \"4MAT\" like response will be required prior to discussion. Engineering leadership, Emotional intelligence, Small group dynamics EGR306 Internship Preparation 1 Units Junior status or greater is required Designed to prepare you for the official internship during your junior summer. Discussion and development of the individual's priorities for their learning contract. Topics include:  resume  and  internship  writing,  finding  an internship, how you will be assessed as an intern, the psychology of the workplace, different types of bosses and working on teams, and the different types of work environment. Internship preparation, Career development, Workplace psychology EGR325 Database Systems 3 Units EGR120 or EGR121 Overview  of  current  database  technologies  with  an emphasis on relational database technology. Introduction to  database  design,  entity  relationship  diagraming,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nand the different types of work environment. Internship preparation, Career development, Workplace psychology EGR325 Database Systems 3 Units EGR120 or EGR121 Overview of current database technologies with an emphasis on relational database technology. Introduction to database design, entity relationship diagraming, structured query language, and stored procedures. Introductory-level SQL, SQL Querying Language EGR329 Computer Architecture 3 Units EGR121 or EGR120 Introduces students to the organization and architecture of computer systems, beginning with the standard von Neumann  model  and  then  moving  forward  to  more recent architectural concepts. Introduction to assembly language programming. Processor Architecture (Von Neumann Model), Data Path, Assembly Language (minor) 7 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions EGR401 Capstone Design 3 Units Multiple paths (e.g., EGR302 & others) The  first  of  a  two  course  senior  capstone  design sequence.  Various  design  topics  may  be  discussed including  equipment  design,  the  design  of  process systems, and economics. Student teams select a project which may involve company sponsorship, and proceed through  the  design  methodology  introduced  in  earlier design  classes,  incorporating  appropriate  engineering standards and multiple realistic constraints. Every project has  a  'customer'  which  requires  the  generation  of  a customer spec. During the sequence students provide detailed schedules for building a prototype system or","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nmethodology introduced in earlier design classes, incorporating appropriate engineering standards and multiple realistic constraints. Every project has a 'customer' which requires the generation of a customer spec. During the sequence students provide detailed schedules for building a prototype system or designing  a  process  and  present  weekly  progress reports.  They  also  produce  technical  specifications, undergo a preliminary design review (PDR), and build a working prototype system if appropriate. Senior Capstone Design Sequence, Engineering Design Methodology, Prototype Development EGR402 Capstone Design and Presentation 3 Units EGR401 A continuation of EGR 401 - Capstone Design. Further development of the project will take place and will involve testing  as  appropriate.  Teams  will  develop  proper documentation for their projects and will appropriately communicate and present the results of their project. A final  presentation  is  given  to  the  public  which  could include members of the engineering advisory council. The presentation will be recorded and included as a part of students' senior portfolios. Capstone Design Project, Testing and Documentation, Professional Project Presentation EGR405 Internship Report and Presentatn 1 Units None listed Students will report on their internship experiences. Each student  will  submit  a  written  report  and  give  an  oral presentation.  Students  will  receive  faculty  and  peer evaluations of their presentations. Internship Experiences, Written Report, Oral Presentation ICS105 Introduction to Global Studies 3 Units None listed","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\ntheir internship experiences. Each student will submit a written report and give an oral presentation. Students will receive faculty and peer evaluations of their presentations. Internship Experiences, Written Report, Oral Presentation ICS105 Introduction to Global Studies 3 Units None listed This  course  addresses  the  basic  elements  of  the Christian faith in the context of participating in God's global agenda. Topics include Christian worldview, the Kingdom of God, the gospel of Jesus Christ, the work of the Holy Spirit, the mission of the Church, and the role of prayer. Christian Worldview, Kingdom of God, Mission of the Church MAT245 Analytcl Geometry and Calculus I 4 Units MAT135, EGR182 or MAT145 Basic  concepts  of  analytical  geometry,  limits  and derivatives,  differentials  and  rates,  integration,  definite and indefinite integrals, differentiation of logarithmic and exponential functions. Differential Calculus, Integral Calculus, Limits, Derivatives (Definition, Rules, and Applications), Integrals (Definition, Fundamental Theorem, and Integration by Substitution) 8 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions MAT403 Linear Algebra 3 Units MAT313 or (MAT245, EGR225) Vector  spaces;  matrices,  rank  and  systems  of  linear equations;  linear  transformation;  similarity  and diagonalization theorems; eigenvectors and eigenvalues. Proof-based Content, Linear Systems, Matrices (Theory of Solutions, Determinants, Inverses), Vector Spaces (Linear Independence, Basis, Dimension, Linear Transformations, Nullity and Rank), Orthogonality,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nVector spaces; matrices, rank and systems of linear equations; linear transformation; similarity and diagonalization theorems; eigenvectors and eigenvalues. Proof-based Content, Linear Systems, Matrices (Theory of Solutions, Determinants, Inverses), Vector Spaces (Linear Independence, Basis, Dimension, Linear Transformations, Nullity and Rank), Orthogonality, Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues PHY201 Physics for Engineers I with Lab 4 Units MAT145, EGR182 or MAT245 This course covers topics such as units, vectors, motion (in one, two and three dimensions), Newton's laws of motion, work, kinetic and potential energy, momentum, impulse,  collisions,  conservation  laws,  dynamics  of rotational motion, equilibrium, gravitation, and periodic motion.  Six  (6)  hours  per  week  of  inquiry-based instruction. Newton's laws of motion, Conservation laws, Dynamics of rotational motion PHY203 Physics for Engineers II wth Lab 4 Units PHY201, MAT245 This course covers topics such as fluids, temperature and ideal gas, electric charge and field, Gauss' Law, electric  potential,  capacitance  and  dielectrics,  current, resistance and electromotive force, direct-current circuits, magnetic field and force, Ampere's and Faraday's laws, electromagnetic  induction,  inductance,  alternating current  circuits,  and  electromagnetic  waves.  Six  (6) hours per week of inquiry-based instruction. Electric charge and field, Direct-current circuits, Electromagnetic induction STA310 Mathematical Statistics I 3 Units (MAT245, EGR120) or EGR121 or STA144 The first semester of a two-semester course providing a systematic development of the theories of probability and","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\nof inquiry-based instruction. Electric charge and field, Direct-current circuits, Electromagnetic induction STA310 Mathematical Statistics I 3 Units (MAT245, EGR120) or EGR121 or STA144 The first semester of a two-semester course providing a systematic development of the theories of probability and statistics. Students learn and use fundamental concepts of  probability  models,  random  variables  and  their distributions,  reduction  of  data,  estimation,  testing  of hypotheses, univariate normal inference, and statistical decision theory. The first semester is required for BA and BS statistics majors of all concentrations. Discrete and continuous random variables, Probability distributions, Random data generation and simulation, Joint distributions, Likelihood concepts, Statistical data visualization and interpretation using R (e.g., histograms, boxplots, bar charts, correlation plots), Interpretation of statistical software outputs (e.g., model summaries, hypothesis testing, ANOVA, regression, graphical diagnostics) 9 of 10 Course # Course Name Units CBU Pre- requisites Course Description Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions STA311 Mathematical Statistics II 3 Units STA310 Second semester course in a systematic development of the theories of probability and statistics. Topics include analysis  of  categorical  data,  multivariate  distributions, nonparametric inference, linear models and analysis of variance. As time permits, the theory underlying Markov chain, Monte Carlo, quasi-likelihood, empirical likelihood, statistical functionals, generalized estimating equations, the jackknife, and the bootstrap are addressed. Hypothesis Testing (Null and Alternative Hypotheses), ANOVA Methods,","CBU CS Transfer Guide\n\ninference, linear models and analysis of variance. As time permits, the theory underlying Markov chain, Monte Carlo, quasi-likelihood, empirical likelihood, statistical functionals, generalized estimating equations, the jackknife, and the bootstrap are addressed. Hypothesis Testing (Null and Alternative Hypotheses), ANOVA Methods, Decision-making using P-values, Linear Regression, Supervised Learning, R Programming, Statistical Software Output Interpretation (e.g., Model Summaries, ANOVA Tables, Regression Outputs, Graphical Diagnostics) 10 of 10","CBU CoE AP Calculus Policy\n\nCBU College of Engineering \nAP Calculus Policies - August 2021 Calc AB = 3 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will earn 4 units towards MAT245 \n• \nAccording to COE policy the student will still be required to take EGR182 Calc AB = 4/5 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will earn 4 units towards MAT245 \n• \nAccording to COE policy the student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the \nstudent 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units \ntowards EGR182. No variance is needed. Calc BC = 3 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will earn 4 units towards MAT255 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will still be required to take MAT245 \n• \nAccording to COE policy the student will still be required to take EGR182 Calc BC = 4/5 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will earn 4 units towards MAT255 \n• \nAccording to CBU policy the student will still be required to take MAT245 \n• \nAccording to COE policy the student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the \nstudent 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units \ntowards EGR182. No variance is needed.","CBU CoE AP Calculus Policy\n\nrequired to take MAT245 • According to COE policy the student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the student 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units towards EGR182. No variance is needed. Calc BC with Calc AB “Subscore” When a student takes Calc BC and receives a Calc AB subscore, their Calc AB subscore will be treated according to the above Calc AB policy except that no university units will be awarded based on the Calc AB subscore • A Calc AB subscore that is higher than the students Calc AB exam results (when applicable) will affect how exemptions are addressed and will not impact earned university units • Example 1: a student receives a 3 on the Calc AB exam results, receives a 3 on their Calc BC exam results as well as a Calc AB subscore of 4 • The student will earn 4 units towards MAT245 for their Calc AB exam result of 3 • The student will earn 4 units towards MAT255 for their Calc BC exam result of 3 • The student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the student 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units towards EGR182. • Example 2: a student never took Calc AB but","CBU CoE AP Calculus Policy\n\nof 3 • The student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the student 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units towards EGR182. • Example 2: a student never took Calc AB but did take Calc BC and received a 3 on the exam and a Calc AB subscore of 4 • The student will earn 4 units towards MAT255 for their Calc BC exam result of 3 • The student will be exempt from taking MAT245 for their Calc AB subscore of 4 but will not gain any units from the Registrar for this class • The student can enroll in EGR182L. EGR182L will earn the student 1 unit and will substitute for EGR182. The student will not earn additional units towards EGR182. Special Note \nDepending on the number of exemptions issued without the association of university units, the \nstudent could be at risk of achieving the total number of Math & Science credits required for \ntheir discipline and the total units required for graduation.  Special attention should be paid \nwhen advising to include additional Math & Science beyond what the flowchart may suggest.","CSDS   Home\n\nWelcome to the Department of Computing, Software & Data Science (CSDS) Connect with us on Discord First thing's first! Jump on to the official CSDS Discord server (an online chat app) to setup your name and expected graduation year. Once there, you will receive important updates and be able to easily communicate with your faculty and classmates. 8/31/26, 1:09 PM CSDS - Home https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Home.aspx 1/2 Many of your classes will even hold official communication there...so be sure to create a Discord account and join our server before you do anything else! Advising Quick Links/Forms Find My Advisor  Authorizations Form  Directed/Indep. Study Forms  Personal Advising Sheets  Transfer Options  Course Variance Forms  Declare/Change Major or Minor  Academic Overload Request  Requirements to Graduate Did you know there are curricular and non-curricular requirements to graduate? Curricular Requirements Extra-Curricular Requirements: 1. CS/SE Major Courses 2. GE (General Education) Course Requirements NOTE: These are built into the flowcharts and advising sheets a. 1. Cross Cultural Requirement 2. Internship Requirement 3. Program Assessment Requirement 8/31/26, 1:09 PM CSDS - Home https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Home.aspx 2/2","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\nCSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 1 of 6 Cross-Cultural Graduation Requirement \nComputing, Software, and Data Sciences (CSDS) Department \nOverview \nThe CSDS cross-cultural graduation requirement is MET when you have completed any ONE of the \nfollowing options: \n1. Intercultural Studies (ICS) Course \n2. Lived Out of Country as Teenager \n3. International Service Trip \n4. Senior Capstone Project w/ Intercultural Integration \n5. Military Foreign Deployment Each of the options has one (1) experiential requirement and one (1) paper requirement, as detailed in the \nsub-sections below. \nSubmission Instructions \nSubmit your cross-cultural requirement by completing the following form before graduation: \n• Cross Cultural Submission Form (https://forms.office.com/r/eHrUP0pUd1) NOTE: The form may ask for additional details, depending on which option you choose, like your overseas \ndates, supervisor contact info, screenshot proof of ICS course, etc., as relevant. \n CSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 2 of 6","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\nThe form may ask for additional details, depending on which option you choose, like your overseas dates, supervisor contact info, screenshot proof of ICS course, etc., as relevant. CSDS @ CBU Cross-Cultural Grad Requirement Revised 4/15/25 Page 2 of 6 Cross-Cultural Option Descriptions \nOption 1: Intercultural Studies (ICS) Course \nExperience Requirement – Approved ICS Course \nComplete any one (1) of the following Intercultural Studies (ICS) courses: \n1. ICS 105 - Introduction to Global Studies (Starting w/ the Fall 2022 offering) \n2. ICS 305 - Global Christianity \n3. ICS 325 - Spiritual Formations for Global Engagement \n4. ICS 375 - Current Events and Movements \n5. ICS 410 - Global and Urban Ministries \n6. ICS 430 - Intercultural Communication and Practice \n7. ICS 431 - Cross-cultural Immersion Note: ICS courses offered by CBU not appearing on this list can NOT be used to meet this requirement. Paper Requirement \nIf the course has a final paper, it can be leveraged and/or modified to meet the requirements in the \nbullet-point below. If the course has no final paper, then a new paper must be completed from scratch \nand submitted containing the following: \n• A 2-3-page (double-spaced) paper summarizing what you learned in the ICS course and how it \nmight be applied when practicing engineering or computer science in a cross-cultural context.","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\npaper must be completed from scratch and submitted containing the following: • A 2-3-page (double-spaced) paper summarizing what you learned in the ICS course and how it might be applied when practicing engineering or computer science in a cross-cultural context. CSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 3 of 6 Option 2: Lived Out of Country as a Teenager \nExperience Requirement – Foreign Residency \nLived in another culture/country for at least one year as a teenager. Paper Requirement \nSubmit a paper with the following: \n• A 2-3-page (double spaced) paper contrasting the cultural similarities/differences between the \nUSA and the non-USA culture you lived in as a teenager that might affect how engineering is \npracticed in that non-USA culture. \n• Some of the many possible areas to explore could include: \n• how one reaches consensus \n• who the stakeholders are \n• the impact of cost and cost differences \n• the types of materials \n• the types of engineering processes \n• differences in labor \n• the impact on the community \n• the nature of the projects that are valued (versus those that may not have value). CSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 4 of 6","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\nengineering processes • differences in labor • the impact on the community • the nature of the projects that are valued (versus those that may not have value). CSDS @ CBU Cross-Cultural Grad Requirement Revised 4/15/25 Page 4 of 6 Option 3: International Service Trip \nExperience Requirement – Eligible Trip \nParticipated in one (1) of the following international service trip options: \n1. International Service Project (ISP) Trip \no Options: https://calbaptist.edu/spiritual-life/serve-global/  \n2. Engineering Service Project (ESP) Trip \n3. A different APPROVED, international service trip \no Email the department chair for approval \nPaper Requirement \nSubmit a paper with the following: \n• A 2-3-page (double spaced) paper in which you reflect on what you did, what you learned, and \nhow you saw it impact your life and/or perceptions as an engineer. CSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 5 of 6","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\n2-3-page (double spaced) paper in which you reflect on what you did, what you learned, and how you saw it impact your life and/or perceptions as an engineer. CSDS @ CBU Cross-Cultural Grad Requirement Revised 4/15/25 Page 5 of 6 Option 4: Senior Capstone Project w/ Intercultural Integration \nExperience Requirement – Eligible Senior Capstone Project \nParticipated in a capstone project that was designated/approved AHEAD OF TIME as eligible for meeting \nthe cross-cultural requirement. For a project to qualify as satisfying the CCE requirement, the design and \nimplementation of the project must incorporate at least one language/culture that is clearly distinct \nfrom the development team’s home language/culture.  The target language(s)/culture(s) should be clearly \ndefined at the beginning of the project and should impact the design process (i.e., the project should have \nrequirements that vary based on the target culture OR require modifications that allow implementation in \nmultiple cultures with minimal or no design changes).","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\nat the beginning of the project and should impact the design process (i.e., the project should have requirements that vary based on the target culture OR require modifications that allow implementation in multiple cultures with minimal or no design changes). Paper Requirement Submit a paper with the following: 1. A 4-page (double spaced) paper meeting the following requirements: a) Discuss at least 3 project requirements that vary (i.e., were impacted) according to the target culture. Requirements could be related to either design or implementation (i.e., rollout) of the project in the target culture(s). • Define the requirements (only include requirements that vary based on target culture). • Define how/why those requirements vary based on the target culture. • Each requirement should be initially defined in terms of the development team’s home language/culture. • Include research done to understand how the requirements would need to be modified based on the target culture(s). • Describe how the requirements were modified for each target culture. b) Discuss how the cultural norms and values of the target culture impacted the design process. How did you determine which requirements would be impacted? How did you research what variations might be required? c) Discuss design decisions that were adapted to allow for implementation in multiple language/cultural contexts (i.e., what aspects of the design were altered so","Cross Cultural Requirement   25 26 and Older Catalogs\n\nyou determine which requirements would be impacted? How did you research what variations might be required? c) Discuss design decisions that were adapted to allow for implementation in multiple language/cultural contexts (i.e., what aspects of the design were altered so that the project could be implemented in multiple cultures without having to be modified OR with very minimal modifications)? d) Discuss what you learned about how the design process might vary depending on the target culture. Discuss what further research would need to be done if your project were to be implemented in a new culture (i.e., a language/culture not considered as part of your project design process). e) Discuss something you learned personally about a culture outside of your home culture. CSDS @ CBU \nCross-Cultural Grad Requirement \nRevised 4/15/25 \nPage 6 of 6 Option 5: Military Foreign Deployment \nExperience Requirement – Foreign Deployment \nServed in the military on deployment in another country (thank you for your service!). Paper Requirement \nSubmit a paper with the following: \n• A 2-page (double spaced) paper reflecting on your interaction with the citizens of the country \n(what you learned, impressions, best ways to communicate, etc.). \n• If you had to do engineering in that country, mention what needs you saw that engineers could \naddress.","Curriculum Flowcharts\n\nCurriculum Flowcharts The following flowcharts can be used to help understand the CS and SE major requirements and pre-requisites for each catalog. Unless you changed it at some point, you should be on the catalog of the academic year you started. The newest flowcharts (starting with 2022-23) include a 2nd flowchart for if you need to start with EGR181 (Elementary Mathematics for Engineering Applications) as your first math course (the assumed starting math is EGR 182: Introductory Mathematics for Engineering Applications). If you have AP credit or have tested into a higher math, make sure to check out our page on Math requirements. NOTE: If you are on the 2019-20 or 2020-21 catalog AND have not completed the 0-unit courses, you should meet with your advisor and most likely convert to the 2021-22 or 2022-23 catalog, as those 0-unit courses are no longer being offered. Computer Science Flowcharts CS 26-27 CS 25-26 CS 24-25 CS 23-24 (Fall 2023 Freshmen) AI/ML Concentration Cybersecurity Concentration General Concentration Planners for above concentrations w/ different Math starting points Data Science Flowcharts DS 26-27 DS 25-26 DS 24-25 Software Engineering Flowcharts SE 22-23 and 23-24 SE 22-23 and 23-24 (starting w/ EGR181) 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 1/5 CS 23-24 (Fall 2022 Freshmen) No flowcharts…planners only CS 22-23 CS 22-23 (starting w/ EGR181)","Curriculum Flowcharts\n\nDS 25-26 DS 24-25 Software Engineering Flowcharts SE 22-23 and 23-24 SE 22-23 and 23-24 (starting w/ EGR181) 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 1/5 CS 23-24 (Fall 2022 Freshmen) No flowcharts…planners only CS 22-23 CS 22-23 (starting w/ EGR181) CS 21-22 CS 20-21 CS 19-20 SE 21-22 SE 20-21 SE 19-20 Quick Reference: CS 26-27 Catalog Flowcharts Major: Computer Science Concentration: Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning (AI/ML) Concentration Major: Computer Science Concentration: Cybersecurity Concentration Major: Data Science Quick Reference: CS 25-26 Catalog Flowcharts CBU_CS_2 CBU_CS_2… Log in Log in CBU_CS_2 CBU_CS_2… Log in Log in CBU_DS_2 CBU_DS_2… Log in Log in 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 2/5 Quick Reference: CS 24-25 Catalog Flowcharts Quick Reference: CS 23-24 Catalog Flowcharts 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 3/5 Quick Reference: Standard CS 22-23 Catalog Flowchart NOTE: More details on the \"Approved Science\" course can be found here. Quick Reference: Standard SE 22-23 Catalog Flowchart NOTE: More details on the \"Approved Science\" course can be found here. 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 4/5 8/31/26, 1:12 PM Curriculum Flowcharts https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/Flowcharts.aspx 5/5","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\nGE (General Education) Requirements\nOfficial GE Requirements\nPlease refer to the following link for OFFICIAL GE requirements before reading on below:\nNow that you've referenced the official requirements above, the summaries below are provided for\nconvenience only and you should always connect with your academic advisor to ensure you are meeting all of\nyour GE requirements:\nA Note on Transitioning Catalogs\nBased on the below summaries, if a student wishes to convert from a 2021-22 catalog (or earlier) to a 2022-23\ncatalog (or newer), there should be no real change in the amount of additional (beyond courses required for\nmajor) GE courses needed to graduate.\n1. 2022-23 and newer GE","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\nto convert from a 2021-22 catalog (or earlier) to a 2022-23 catalog (or newer), there should be no real change in the amount of additional (beyond courses required for major) GE courses needed to graduate. 1. 2022-23 and newer GE 1. 2025-26 CS/DS GE Summary (New GE) 2. 2022-23 CS/SE GE Summary (New GE) 3. 2021-22 CS/SE GE Summary (Old GE)  2025-26 and Newer CS/DS GE Summary (New GE) Under the new GE, the number of GE-specific units remains the same (see below) as in the Old GE catalog. CS and SE students will still need 18-20 units of GE on top of their major requirements. The list below shows the GE requirements covered by CS/SE major requirements (in green) and the GE requirements that must be taken as standalone (i.e., non-major) courses (note that these standalone GE courses ARE included in the CS/SE flowcharts): Foundations (6-12 units) ENGR1130/1130E (3/4 units) – Needed ENGR1230/1230E (3/4 units) – Needed Mathematics – Met by major (MATH 2450) Personal World (3 units) 8/31/26, 1:10 PM GE (General Education) Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/GERequirements.aspx 1/4 Technology or Foreign Language – Met by major (CSCI****) Biblical & Theological Core (9 units) 3 units – Met by major (ICST1050) 3 units – Met by major (CHST1300) 3 units – Met by major (CHST3700) Social World (6 units) Communication","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\nor Foreign Language – Met by major (CSCI****) Biblical & Theological Core (9 units) 3 units – Met by major (ICST1050) 3 units – Met by major (CHST1300) 3 units – Met by major (CHST3700) Social World (6 units) Communication Studies – Met by major (ENGR3020) Behavioral Sciences – Met by major (ENGR3040, 3060, and 4050) Natural World (4 units) Lab Science – Met by major (PHYS 2010) Civic World (6 units) 6 units – Needed ​NOTE: This can be fulfilled by taking any 2 (two) 3-unit course in the following list: WLDV1610, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, LAWT* and more Cultural World (3 units) English – Met by major (ENGR4010) Creative World (3 units) Design – Met by major (ENGR1020)","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\nbe fulfilled by taking any 2 (two) 3-unit course in the following list: WLDV1610, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, LAWT* and more Cultural World (3 units) English – Met by major (ENGR4010) Creative World (3 units) Design – Met by major (ENGR1020) 2022-23, 2023-24 and 2024-25 CS/SE GE Summary (New GE) Under the new GE, the number of GE-specific units remains the same (see below) as in the Old GE catalog. CS and SE students will still need 18-20 units of GE on top of their major requirements. The list below shows the GE requirements covered by CS/SE major requirements (in green) and the GE requirements that must be taken as standalone (i.e., non-major) courses (note that these standalone GE courses ARE included in the CS/SE flowcharts): Foundations (6-12 units) ENGR1130/1130E (3/4 units) – Needed ENGR1230/1230E (3/4 units) – Needed Mathematics – Met by major (MATH 2450) Personal World (3 units) Technology or Foreign Language – Met by major (CSCI****) Biblical & Theological Core (9 units) 3 units – Met by major (ICST1050) 6 units – Needed ​NOTE: This can be fulfilled by taking any 2 (two) 3-unit CHST* or ICST* courses (other than ICST1050 b/c you are already taking it) Social World (6 units) Communication Studies – Met by major (ENGR3020) Behavioral Sciences – Met by major (ENGR3040, 3060, and 4050)","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\nby taking any 2 (two) 3-unit CHST* or ICST* courses (other than ICST1050 b/c you are already taking it) Social World (6 units) Communication Studies – Met by major (ENGR3020) Behavioral Sciences – Met by major (ENGR3040, 3060, and 4050) Natural World (4 units) Lab Science – Met by major (PHYS 2010) Civic World (6 units) 8/31/26, 1:10 PM GE (General Education) Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/GERequirements.aspx 2/4 6 units – Needed ​NOTE: This can be fulfilled by taking any 2 (two) 3-unit course in the following list: WLDV161, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, and more Cultural World (3 units) English – Met by major (ENGR4010) Creative World (3 units) Design – Met by major (ENGR1020)","GE (General Education) Requirements\n\ncan be fulfilled by taking any 2 (two) 3-unit course in the following list: WLDV161, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, and more Cultural World (3 units) English – Met by major (ENGR4010) Creative World (3 units) Design – Met by major (ENGR1020) 2021-22 CS/SE GE Summary (Old GE)\nUnder the previous GE, Computer Science & Software Engineering students needed 18-20 units of GE on top of\ntheir major requirements:\n3-4 units Competency (ENG113/113E)\n3-4 units Foundation (ENG123/123E)\n6 units Biblical & Theological Core\n6 units Political World\nBelow is a list of GE requirement areas and Engineering courses that will meet each of the GE areas required. This\nlist does not include all CBU courses that will meet GE areas. Transfer coursework that is substituted for a major\nclass listed below MAY NOT satisfy a GE requirement. For questions on GE requirements, contact your academic\nadvisor (for GE questions).\n8/31/26, 1:10 PM\nGE (General Education) Requirements\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/GERequirements.aspx\n3/4 8/31/26, 1:10 PM\nGE (General Education) Requirements\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/GERequirements.aspx\n4/4","Math Placement\n\nMath Placement While EGR 182 is the preferred starting math course, flowcharts for 4-year graduation plans exist for students starting with math as early as EGR 181. Math Placement via High School Courses The following list informs how High School course work and performance automatically places students into CBU math coursework: B- or better in anything before Pre-Calc grants access to EGR181 B- or better in Pre-Calc grants access to EGR182 B- or better in Calc AB grants access to MAT145 B- or better in Calc BC grants access to MAT245 NOTE: If you think you were placed into the wrong math class based on your high school history, contact your student success coach (Star Wildes); otherwise, you need to take the placement exam (see below) for a chance at getting placed into a higher math class. Math Placement via Placement Exams To place out of EGR181 and into EGR182, you must complete the Placement Exam called Algorithmic Advanced Algebra (instructions found here). Math Placement via AP Exam Scores The following document details the math placement details for various AP Math scenarios. In short, note that EGR 182 (Intro Math for EGR Applications), which is a \"lower\" math than Calc 1 and 2 is a required course in the CS/SE curriculum. Note that if you come into a CSDS program","Math Placement\n\nMath scenarios. In short, note that EGR 182 (Intro Math for EGR Applications), which is a \"lower\" math than Calc 1 and 2 is a required course in the CS/SE curriculum. Note that if you come into a CSDS program with MAT 245 credit (either via a transferred course or AP credit), although you may not have to take all 4-units of EGR 182: you will still have to take a 1-unit EGR 182L (lab) you may have to replace the 3 units not taken up by EGR 182 with something (anything) else to meet your 124- minimum units to graduate NOTE: The above nuances are captured in the personal advising sheets 8/31/26, 1:09 PM\nMath Placement\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/MathPlacement.aspx\n1/2\n \n1\n/ 2\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFont is already loaded.\n\n8/31/26, 1:09 PM\nMath Placement\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/MathPlacement.aspx\n2/2","Non Course Requirements\n\nNon-Course Requirements In addition to the specific courses you need to complete to graduate (see the CS/SE flowcharts or personal advising sheets), you also need to fulfill requirements in the following three (3) areas to graduate: See below for more details on each of these. 1. Cross Cultural Requirement 2. Internship Requirement 3. Program Assessment Requirement  1 - Cross Cultural Requirement 2026-27 Catalog and Newer Students on the 2026-27 CS catalog and newer do NOT need to do anything outside take their required courses to complete the Cross-Cultural Graduation Requirement as the Cross-Cultural requirement will be evaluated directly by CHST 1300/3700 instructors in those required courses and will NOT require an additional submission (i.e., if you pass CHST 1300/3700, you will automatically have your Cross-Cultural Requirement fulfilled). 2025-26 Catalogs and Older 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 1/6 The following document describes in detail the cross-cultural requirement (note that there are multiple options, some of which you may be close to completing if you grew up overseas or have been on an international mission trip). All cross-cultural requirement submissions MUST be made through the following form (i.e., do NOT e-mail anyone): Cross Cultural Submission Form (https://forms.office.com/r/eHrUP0pUd1) 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 2/6   11 Cross Cultural Requirement.pdf Cross Cultural Requirement.pdf Log in Log in Download Sign up","Non Course Requirements\n\nMUST be made through the following form (i.e., do NOT e-mail anyone): Cross Cultural Submission Form (https://forms.office.com/r/eHrUP0pUd1) 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 2/6   11 Cross Cultural Requirement.pdf Cross Cultural Requirement.pdf Log in Log in Download Sign up Sign up 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 3/6 If you elect to choose the option where you take one of the prescribed ICS courses, please note that these courses DO COUNT as 3 of the units you do need toward your 6 units of Biblical and Theological Core GE requirement. Thus, for most students, it is prudent to make sure you enroll in one of the prescribed ICS courses sooner rather than later. 2 - Internship Requirement All College of Engineering students must complete a minimum of 200 hours of internship before being able to graduate (this typically happens during the summer between a student's Junior and Senior years). The official process and requirements are detailed in the document below in the internship packet PDF. Enrollment into ENGR 4050 The following details the basic process of being able to enroll into ENGR 4050: 1. Student completes ENGR 3060 and majority of their junior-level courses (i.e., internship is meant to be taken in the summer before your last full year at CBU)","Non Course Requirements\n\ndetails the basic process of being able to enroll into ENGR 4050: 1. Student completes ENGR 3060 and majority of their junior-level courses (i.e., internship is meant to be taken in the summer before your last full year at CBU) 2. Student secures internship in their field and submits an internship application (see pdf below for instructions/links) a. Do NOT e-mail your request to department chair 3. Once approved, student will be put on a list to register for ENGR 4050 a. CoE internship office will add ENGR 4050 authorizations for eligible students two months prior to the eligible semester (typically July and November) i. Eligible students are those who have completed at least 100 of their 200 required hours ii. Student will be e-mailed when they have been approved to register for ENGR 4050 b. NOTE: When registering for other courses, students should plan to keep a time slot open for preferably two different sections of ENGR 4050 so that they can be sure to get into one that fits their schedule when the opportunity arises 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 4/6 3 - Program Assessment Requirement The following program requirement will be fulfilled during your last semester at CBU and involves data gathering that helps us to assess our program, based on your feedback and performance.","Non Course Requirements\n\nPM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 4/6 3 - Program Assessment Requirement The following program requirement will be fulfilled during your last semester at CBU and involves data gathering that helps us to assess our program, based on your feedback and performance. In short, you will have two requirements: 1. Complete the MFT CS exam (will be scheduled in your ENGR 4010/4020 class during the last 4-6 weeks of your last semester) 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 5/6 a. This test is typically administered through the ENGR 4020 capstone course but can be taken by appointment by contacting the CSDS department chair if graduating in the Fall (please reach out by early November to schedule your exam). b. The first test is provided at no cost to the student by the department.  If a student fails to score in the 10th percentile or higher then they will be required to take the test again at personal expense (as of April 2026 the cost is $32.50 per sitting) until they successfully score in the 10th percentile or higher. c. This score is not used to calculate a grade for any course.  The 10th percentile requirement is simply there to motivate students to do their best so that results will truly reflect the effectiveness of the programs offered at CBU.","Non Course Requirements\n\nThis score is not used to calculate a grade for any course. The 10th percentile requirement is simply there to motivate students to do their best so that results will truly reflect the effectiveness of the programs offered at CBU. 2. Complete the 2026 Senior Exit Survey (https://forms.office.com/r/xaBuRtHFBP) a. NOTE: You will submit your scores from the MFT CS exam to this survey. b. Make sure to complete this survey about 1-2 weeks before you intend to graduate. 8/31/26, 1:19 PM Non-Course Requirements https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/NonCourseGradRequirements.aspx 6/6","Personal Advising Sheets\n\nPersonal Advising Sheets The following advising sheet templates can be used to help you plan out and re-plan your academic career in the CSDS department at CBU. The spreadsheets have the following baked into each template: Visual feedback (red) if a course is not completed or planned Visual feedback (red) if a pre-requisite or co-requisite is not met (based on your plan) Visual feedback for which semester a course is offered Templates are available for both Computer Science (CS) and Software Engineering (SE) majors for the 2022-23 catalog (although previous catalogs should be similar or equivalent), assuming various different starting points for math. A generic starting point is offered for transfer students. More details are provided below for each template. If you have AP credit or have tested into a higher math, make sure to check out our page on Math requirements to know what is required of you. Modern Catalogs (2023 and later) The following links contain advising sheets (4-year planners) for Computer Science and Data Science for various scenarios, including the Machine Learning or Cyber Security concentrations (CS) and various starting points with math; start with one of the sheets/planners at the following links: 2026-27 (CS & DS) 2025-26 (CS & DS) 2024-25 (CS & DS) 2023-24 (CS)  8/31/26, 1:13 PM Personal Advising Sheets https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx 1/6 You","Personal Advising Sheets\n\nand various starting points with math; start with one of the sheets/planners at the following links: 2026-27 (CS & DS) 2025-26 (CS & DS) 2024-25 (CS & DS) 2023-24 (CS)  8/31/26, 1:13 PM Personal Advising Sheets https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx 1/6 You started the CS major in Fall 2023 or later You started the CS (or old SE) major before Fall 2023 (e.g., Fall 2022 Freshmen) 22-23 Catalog The following links contain advising sheets (4- year planners) for various scenarios, including the Computer Science and Software Engineering Majors and various starting points with math. NOTE: More details on the \"Approved Science\" course can be found here. 2022-23 CS Advising Sheet Templates Start with one of the following templates if you are: starting w/ EGR 181 (Elementary Math for EGR) starting w/ EGR 182 (Intro Math for EGR) starting w/ MAT 245 (Calc 1) starting w/ MAT 255 (Calc 2) a transfer student transferring in a lot of GEs 2022-23 SE Advising Sheet Templates Start with one of the following templates if you are: starting w/ EGR 181 (Elementary Math for EGR) starting w/ EGR 182 (Intro Math for EGR) starting w/ MAT 245 (Calc 1) starting w/ MAT 255 (Calc 2) Advisor View Tab The Advisor View tab in the Excel sheet will typically be filled out or confirmed by your advisor,","Personal Advising Sheets\n\nw/ EGR 182 (Intro Math for EGR) starting w/ MAT 245 (Calc 1) starting w/ MAT 255 (Calc 2) Advisor View Tab The Advisor View tab in the Excel sheet will typically be filled out or confirmed by your advisor, but we provide the tool for you as a pre-advising exercise, and for your convenience, to attempt your own plan. The following screen capture shows some of the essential elements: 8/31/26, 1:13 PM Personal Advising Sheets https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx 2/6 8/31/26, 1:13 PM Personal Advising Sheets https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx 3/6 Student View Tab\nThe Student View tab filters through the work you did in the Advisor View tab and displays a clean summary of your plan, as seen in the following screen\ncapture:\n8/31/26, 1:13 PM\nPersonal Advising Sheets\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx\n4/6\n 8/31/26, 1:13 PM\nPersonal Advising Sheets\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx\n5/6 8/31/26, 1:13 PM\nPersonal Advising Sheets\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/AdvisingSheets.aspx\n6/6","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nProgram & Catalog Changes This page details important changes from year to year, introduced in various versions of the catalog. When considering whether to change to a newer catalog, it is important to consider this information. Quick Links Computer/Data Science 2025-26 Computer Science 2024-25 Data Science 2024-25 Computer Science 2023-24 Computer Science 2022-23 Computer Science 2021-22 Computer Science & Data Science: 2025-26 Catalog Curriculum changes were made by the College of Engineering that impact both the Computer Science and Data Science program for the 2025-26 catalog. For both programs prior to the 2025-26 catalog, students were allowed to take electives of their choice to fulfill the remaining 6-units of CBU General Education (GE) requirements related to “Biblical & Theological Core”. Starting in the 2025-26 catalog, all College of Engineering programs require students to fulfill those 6 units by taking the following two (2) courses: 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 1/8 CST 130 - Overview of the Bible CST 370 - The Christian Faith Major: Computer Science Concentration: Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning (AI/ML) Concentration Major: Data Science Computer Science: 2024-25 Catalog The ABET CAC criteria’s curriculum requirements for natural sciences recently changed: 2023-24 CAC CS Criteria: At least six semester credit hours (or equivalent) in natural science course work intended for science and engineering majors. This course","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nComputer Science: 2024-25 Catalog The ABET CAC criteria’s curriculum requirements for natural sciences recently changed: 2023-24 CAC CS Criteria: At least six semester credit hours (or equivalent) in natural science course work intended for science and engineering majors. This course work must develop an understanding of the scientific method and must include laboratory work. 2024-25 CAC CS Criteria: Science: Coursework that develops and applies the scientific method in a non-computing area. CBU_CS_25-26_4_Year_Plan_Start_EG CBU_CS_25-26_4_Year_Plan_Start_EG… Log in Log in CBU_DS_25-26_4_Year_Plan_Start_EG CBU_DS_25-26_4_Year_Plan_Start_EG… Log in Log in 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 2/8 In response to ABET softening their requirements from 6 units (including lab work) to no specific unit or lab requirements, the natural science requirements have been modified and eased to maintain a broad scientific exposure, while also increasing flexibility for student scheduling. Thus, in the 2024-25 catalog, PHY 203 (Physics 2) is being removed as a requirement and being replaced with a natural science elective, including any of the following: BIO 114 – General Botany w/ Lab (4 units) BIO 146 – General Biology I w/ Lab (4 units) BIO 153+153L – Anatomy and Physiology I w/ Lab (4 units) CHE 112+112L – Organic and Biochem. for Health Sciences w/ Lab (4 units) CHE 115+115L – General Chemistry I w/ Lab (4 units) PHY 113+113L – Astronomy w/","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nunits) BIO 153+153L – Anatomy and Physiology I w/ Lab (4 units) CHE 112+112L – Organic and Biochem. for Health Sciences w/ Lab (4 units) CHE 115+115L – General Chemistry I w/ Lab (4 units) PHY 113+113L – Astronomy w/ Lab (4 units) PHY 203 – Physics for Engineers II w/ Lab (4 units) SCI 120 – Marine Science w/ Lab (4 units) SCI 205 – Introduction to Forensic Science w/ Lab (4 units) Data Science: 2024-25 Catalog The Data Science major was introduced in the 2024 catalog as a new major. If you look at 2024-25 Computer Science major with the AI/ML concentration, it differs by making the following changes: Remove the following courses (32 units): SW Engineering Core: CSC 321 - Web App Development (3 units) CSC 322 - Mobile App Development (3 units) CS Core: CSC 313 - Graphics & Visual Computing (3 units) CSC 415 - Theory of Computation & Compilers (3 units) EGR 329 - Computer Architecture (3 units) EGR 425 - Real Time & Embedded Development (3 units) Other: CSC 421 - Artificial Intelligence (3 units) CSC 431 - Info Security & Computer Forensics (3 units) PHY 201 - Physics for Engineers 1 w/ Lab (4 units) 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 3/8 NSE - Natural Science Elective (4 units) Add","Program & Catalog Changes\n\n(3 units) CSC 431 - Info Security & Computer Forensics (3 units) PHY 201 - Physics for Engineers 1 w/ Lab (4 units) 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 3/8 NSE - Natural Science Elective (4 units) Add the following courses (30 units): Math Core: MAT 255 - Analytical Geometry & Calc II (4 units) ML Core: CSC 221 - Data Wrangling (3 units) Stats Core: STA 205 - Applied Linear Regression (3 units) STA 362 - Advanced Statistical Computing (3 units) STA 360 - Data Visualization (3 units) STA 364 - Applied Statistical Methodology (3 units) GIS Core: CSC 441 - Essentials of GIS (3 units) CSC 442 - Intermediate GIS (3 units) CSC 443 - Advanced GIS (3 units) Other: Elective - Any CBU/Transfer Credit (2 units) NOTE: The first year of the CS and DS majors are exactly the same, so it is easy to switch majors during the first year Computer Science: 2023-24 Catalog The most significant changes to the CS program were introduced in the 2023-24 catalog and represent more than a year’s worth of work, discussion and feedback from all CSDS faculty/lecturers (via regular department meetings), relevant non-Engineering Deans and department Chairs (via ad- hoc meetings) and industry professionals (through multiple Advisory Council meetings). Prior to these curriculum changes, the Computing, Software","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nworth of work, discussion and feedback from all CSDS faculty/lecturers (via regular department meetings), relevant non-Engineering Deans and department Chairs (via ad- hoc meetings) and industry professionals (through multiple Advisory Council meetings). Prior to these curriculum changes, the Computing, Software and Data Sciences (CSDS) department managed two programs: 1) this ABET-accredited CS program, and 2) a non-ABET-accredited Software Engineering program. The two programs shared a significant overlap and only differed in six (6) upper-division courses. To enhance the CS program and consolidate resources, the Software Engineering program (which had far fewer students than the CS program) was sunset; while four courses were eliminated due to the redundancy of material being taught within the Software Engineering program, Mobile App Development and Web App Development were both brought into the CS program. 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 4/8 As a high-level summary, from the 2022-23 to 2023-24 CS program catalog, 3 CS courses were eliminated, 2 CS courses were merged into 1 CS course, 9 CS courses were added, 3 math courses were eliminated and 3 math courses were added to reorganize the CS program into four (4) cores and two (2) optional concentrations of knowledge/practice: CS Major Cores: CS Major Concentrations: Specific changes are included below: Overall Unit Changes The overall net change in units is +2 2022-23","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nadded to reorganize the CS program into four (4) cores and two (2) optional concentrations of knowledge/practice: CS Major Cores: CS Major Concentrations: Specific changes are included below: Overall Unit Changes The overall net change in units is +2 2022-23 catalog was 124 units 2023-24 catalog is 126 units Core CS/EGR Course Changes New Additions from Scratch: Software Engineering (Core) Course: CSC 323 - Cloud Computing and Application Development Machine Learning (Concentration) Courses: CSC 422 - Machine and Deep Learning CSC 423 - Data Mining CSC 424 - Natural Language Processing Cybersecurity (Concentration) Courses: CSC 432 - Network Traffic Analysis CSC 433 - Cyber Security Incident Response and Recovery CSC 434 - Secure System Principles New Additions from the Software Engineering Major: Software Engineering (Core) Courses: CSC 322 – Mobile App Dev (new to CS) NOTE: Renumbered from EGR 423 CSC 321 – Web App Dev (new to CS) NOTE: Renumbered from EGR 424 Merges: 1. Programming & Algorithms 2. Software Engineering 3. Computing Systems Hierarchy & Theory 4. Math & Science 1. Machine Learning & AI 2. Cybersecurity 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 5/8 Computer Science (Core) Course(s): CSC 415 - Compiler and Theory of Computation (new course combining the two courses below) CSC 311 – Compiler Design CSC 414 – Theory of Computation Eliminations: CSC","Program & Catalog Changes\n\n1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 5/8 Computer Science (Core) Course(s): CSC 415 - Compiler and Theory of Computation (new course combining the two courses below) CSC 311 – Compiler Design CSC 414 – Theory of Computation Eliminations: CSC 411 – Social & Professional Issues EGR 223 – SWE Approach to Human-Computer-Interface (HCI) EGR 324 – Engineering Economics Course Number Changes: CSC 431 – Info Security & Computer Forensics (primer/teaser to Cyber security concentration) NOTE: Renumbered from CSC 413 CSC 421 – Artificial Intelligence (primer/teaser to ML concentration) NOTE: Renumbered from CSC 412 (formerly named Intelligent Systems) Math and Science Course Changes New Additions: STA 310 - Mathematical Statistics I STA 311 - Mathematical Statistics II MAT 403 - Linear Algebra Eliminate: MAT 255 – Analytical Geo. And Calc 2 EGR 305 – Engineering Stats EGR 328 – Numerical Methods for Computing Approved Science (2 units) Flow, Ordering and Pre-requisite Changes To enhance and optimize the student’s learning experience, the order in which some courses are taken and a number of pre-requisites have been updated. Computer Science: 2022-23 Catalog 1. Minor University-Driven Changes (New in 2022-23 Catalog) Modifications were made due to the university making changes to the General Education requirements starting with the 2022-23 catalog; these changes only had a very small effect on engineering students. Previously, for","Program & Catalog Changes\n\n1. Minor University-Driven Changes (New in 2022-23 Catalog) Modifications were made due to the university making changes to the General Education requirements starting with the 2022-23 catalog; these changes only had a very small effect on engineering students. Previously, for the engineering programs, students needed 18 additional units of GE courses that were not covered by engineering courses – ENG113 and ENG123 (English 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 6/8 composition courses), 6 units of Biblical and Theological Core (9 units were required but EGR101 covered 3 units), and 6 units from the Political world (essentially one political science course and one history course). With the change, students still need 18 additional units, but the organization has changed – ENG113 and ENG123, 6 units from the Biblical and Theological Core (9 units required but 3 units covered by ICS105), 6 units from the Civic World (courses can be from economics, political science, history, and law). The new and old GE requirements can be found here.","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nthe Biblical and Theological Core (9 units required but 3 units covered by ICS105), 6 units from the Civic World (courses can be from economics, political science, history, and law). The new and old GE requirements can be found here. 2. Moderate CoE-Driven Changes (New in 2022-23 Catalog) Modifications were made due to the College of Engineering (CoE) at California Baptist University (CBU) making changes to some 1st year requirements. Previously, students during their first year took EGR101 (Engineering from a Christian Worldview – 4 units) along with EGR103 (Engineering Service – 1 unit) and EGR192 (Engineering Seminar – 1 unit). Beginning with the 2022-23 catalog, EGR101 was reduced to 3 units, EGR103 and EGR192 were no longer required, and ICS105 (Introduction to Global Studies – 3 units) was added as a requirement. Much of the focused Biblical material was removed from EGR101, the service component of EGR103 was added to EGR101 and EGR102, and information about various engineering disciplines (as was covered in EGR192) was added to EGR101. The background on this change is that the university allowed EGR101 to count towards the General Education requirement in the area of Biblical and Theological Core. However, only 1.5 hours of the course was really focused on Biblical material. Other courses that fulfilled this GE credit would have a full 3","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nto count towards the General Education requirement in the area of Biblical and Theological Core. However, only 1.5 hours of the course was really focused on Biblical material. Other courses that fulfilled this GE credit would have a full 3 hours focused on Biblical material. With this change, EGR101 no longer counts towards a GE requirement, but ICS105 does count towards that requirement. There was no net change in units due to these changes. Computer Science: 2021-22 Catalog 1. Moderate University-Driven Changes (New in 2020-21 Catalog) Modifications were made due to the university no longer allowing zero-unit courses to be required. Beginning in the 2020-21 catalog, EGR202 (Worldview Reflection I), EGR404 (Worldview Reflection II), and EGR406 (Senior Portfolio) were removed as requirements. EGR390 (Internship) was removed and replaced with a general requirement to “Complete pre-approved 200 hours of supervised internship.” EGR403 (FE Exam) was removed and replaced with a general requirement to “Complete the program assessment exam requirement.” Students must now score in the top 10% of the Computer Science Major Field Test (MFT) and complete an exit survey shortly before graduation. Since we are using this as an evaluation option for the program, and not the student, the minimum score of placing in the 10th percentile is simply to ensure students make an honest effort to do well on","Program & Catalog Changes\n\nbefore graduation. Since we are using this as an evaluation option for the program, and not the student, the minimum score of placing in the 10th percentile is simply to ensure students make an honest effort to do well on the CS MFT exam. The MFT exam is a proctored examination that is administered annually, typically during one meeting time of the Spring EGR402 Capstone Design & Presentation course. 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 7/8 8/31/26, 1:20 PM Program & Catalog Changes https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/ProgramChanges.aspx 8/8","Transfer Options\n\nTransfer Options\nTransfer Options\nIf you want to know if a course you've already taken will transfer to your CS/SE major at CBU, OR if you want to find a course to transfer in for CS/SE credit:\nSearch for courses on transferology.com\nIf the course you've taken or hope to take is not on transferology.com, then you may:\nAttempt to transfer a course with no known agreement\nFinding Courses that Automatically Transfer to CBU Courses\nIf you're interested in transferring a course in to help ease your unit load, then the easiest way is to use the transferology.com website. Once there, register\nfor a free account and then you can select courses at CBU for which you hope to find equivalent courses at other colleges:\n8/31/26, 1:11 PM\nTransfer Options\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx\n1/7\n Once you've selected courses, you can \"Search for Matches\":\nFind a Replacement Course\n8/31/26, 1:11 PM\nTransfer Options\nhttps://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx\n2/7\n Clicking on a school will show you any courses that are known to automatically transfer over to the specific CBU courses you selected:","Transfer Options\n\ncourses, you can \"Search for Matches\": Find a Replacement Course 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 2/7 Clicking on a school will show you any courses that are known to automatically transfer over to the specific CBU courses you selected: Search results for \"EGR121\" and \"EGR122\" Transferring Courses with no Known Transfer Agreement If you've found a course you hope to be a match for a specific CSDS course you're required to take, but there is no transfer agreement found on transferology.com, there still might be a chance to get it transferred! In that case: 1. Before taking the course: 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 3/7 a. Check for Content: Check out the transfer guide below which provides tips of what to look for (these are the main things we look for in determining if a transfer course would be a good match): i. Find the CBU course you want credit for and review the “Recommended Topics to Look for in Transfer Course Descriptions” ii. Find a course description at another school and look for some of the “Recommended Topics” 1. If you find one or more of the Recommended Topics, continue on to the next step 2. If NONE of the Recommended Topics are mentioned, it's best to look for another course option b. Check the School: Ensure","Transfer Options\n\nTopics” 1. If you find one or more of the Recommended Topics, continue on to the next step 2. If NONE of the Recommended Topics are mentioned, it's best to look for another course option b. Check the School: Ensure the school in which you hope to transfer credit: i. Is a non-profit school and is regionally accredited 1. Regional Accreditation Check: You can check accreditation status on the Council for Higher Education Accreditation website a. Search for the school's name in the main search bar on the home page (Institutions Directory should be selected) and bring up the school's page b. If the school's page shows a section called “Institutional Accreditation” with an accreditor listed, it is regionally accredited i. If you can't find the school, it's likely not accredited and you should find another option 2. Non-Profit Check: You can check non-profit status on the National Center for Educational Statistics website a. Search by the “Name of School” and bring up the school's page b. If the school's “Type” field shows “Public” or “Private not-for-profit”, it is a non-profit ii. Offers “semester” units 1. It's okay if they offer compacted terms, but their normal terms should be in semesters and NOT quarters c. Confirm Informally w/ CSDS Chair: First obtain a syllabus and send it in an e-mail","Transfer Options\n\nnon-profit ii. Offers “semester” units 1. It's okay if they offer compacted terms, but their normal terms should be in semesters and NOT quarters c. Confirm Informally w/ CSDS Chair: First obtain a syllabus and send it in an e-mail to the CSDS Chair asking if the external course's content looks equivalent to our CBU course. i. In your e-mail to the chair, please include: 1. CBU course you want credit for 2. Transfer school name and unit type (semester vs. quarters) 3. Transfer Course name, number, units and description of potential transfer course 4. Link to course description on the school's website 5. If you are able to obtain it, a link to syllabus and/or attached syllabus (you may need to request from the school) ii. The CSDS Chair will consult with faculty and may informally approve the course, but only for content. iii. The student would then need to check with the registrar's office to ensure CBU would accept the course (as general credit). d. (Optional) Complete Transfer Request Form: If and only if the CSDS Chair has given you an informal go-ahead over e-mail: i. If You may obtain FORMAL approval from the registrar by completing the following two forms: 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 4/7","Transfer Options\n\nRequest Form: If and only if the CSDS Chair has given you an informal go-ahead over e-mail: i. If You may obtain FORMAL approval from the registrar by completing the following two forms: 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 4/7 NOTE: This process does not apply to GE courses as the CSDS department has no power to determine if an external course can count toward GE credit. You should instead contact your student success coach, Trent Troncone (SSC). 1. https://portal.laserfiche.com/m0921/forms/XFRCourseApproval (Official CBU Registrar Form) 2. https://forms.office.com/r/GshK6uzMaF (Official CSDS Form) ii. NOTE: The registrar will only pre-approve a lower-division transfer course for a lower-division CBU course, or an upper-division transfer course for an upper-division CBU course; however, if the CSDS Chair gave you informal approval (see 1.c above) and the course is able to transfer to CBU as general credit (see 1.b above), you can still transfer the course via the steps in 2.c.ii below. 2. AFTER successfully completing the course: a. Request Credit Transfer to CBU: Ask the school at which you took the transfer course to send your course/grade to CBU b. See Transferred Credit in CBU Transcript: Wait for the credit to show up in your unofficial transcript c. Finalize Transfer Request: If you: i. submitted a Transfer Request Form (see 1.d above) and it was APPROVED, your","Transfer Options\n\nto CBU b. See Transferred Credit in CBU Transcript: Wait for the credit to show up in your unofficial transcript c. Finalize Transfer Request: If you: i. submitted a Transfer Request Form (see 1.d above) and it was APPROVED, your degree guide should automatically update to show your CSDS degree requirement as being met with no further action needed ii. did not submit a Transfer Request Form (see 1.d above) or if it was REJECTED, but you still received informal approval by the CSDS Chair (see 1.c above) 1. Follow the official variance request process, detailed here (submit 2 forms) 2. The registrar's office would then send the course back to the CSDS Chair for formal approval and your degree guide should eventually get updated by the registrar to show your CSDS degree requirement as being met a. NOTE: This last step won't happen until you complete the course and it gets transferred back in as general credit. CBU CSDS Course Transfer Guide The following document can be used as a guide for what to look for in a potential transfer course; find the CBU course you are looking to earn credit for and then look for the “Recommended Topics” in the course description of the potential transfer course. 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 5/7 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer","Transfer Options\n\ntransfer course; find the CBU course you are looking to earn credit for and then look for the “Recommended Topics” in the course description of the potential transfer course. 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 5/7 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 6/7 8/31/26, 1:11 PM Transfer Options https://calbaptist.sharepoint.com/sites/CSDS/SitePages/TransferOptions.aspx 7/7","CBU CS 26 27 4 Year Plan Start ENGR1810 Cybersecurity CONC\n\nLEGEND B.S. in Computer Science, CYBERSECURITY Concentration (Starting Math: ENGR1810) - 2026/2027 Catalog (130 Units) Updated 5/19/26 CHST 1300 (3) Overview of the Bible ENGR 2250 (3) Discrete Structures Fall 2027 16 Units ENGR 2260 (3) OS and Networking Civic World (3) * PHYS 2010 (4) Phy for Engrs I with Lab Spring 2028 16 Units GNST 1000 (1) First Year Experience (freshmen only) ICST1050 (3) Intro to Global Studies (ENGR Section) ENGR 1010 (3) Engr from Christian Worldview ENGL 1130/E (3/4) ‡ Composition Fall 2026 14/15 Units ENGR 1220 (3) † Visualization Languages I ENGR 1020 (4) Intro to Engr Design ENGL 1230/E (3/4) ‡ Intermediate Composition Spring 2027 17/18 Units ENGR 3250 (3) Database Design CSCI 3220 (3) Mobile Application Development ENGR 3060 (1) Internship Preparation ** Natural Science Elective (4) Fall 2028 16 Units ENGR 3020 (3) Engr Design and Doc (CSDS Section) CSCI 3230 (3) Cloud Computing & App Dev ENGR 3040 (1) Leadership Cohort (CSDS Section) CSCI 4210 (3) Artificial Int