 2220 ENGL 1130 ENGR 2270 ENGR 2220 ENGR 2220 ENGR 2220 MATH 2450 STAT3100 ENGR 2250 ENGR 2250 MATH 2450 MATH 2450 CSCI 3210 CSCI 3220 OR Junior ENGR 2260 ENGR 2270 ENGR 2220 ENGR 3020 ENGR 2250 ENGR 2270 200 Hrs ENGR 1200 /ENGR 1210 STAT3100 ENGR 1200 /ENGR 1210 STAT3100 ENGR 4010 ENGR 2270 ENGR 2220 ENGR 3290 ENGR 1200","CBU CS 26 27 4 Year Plan Start STAT3100 ML CONC\n\nCSCI 3210 CSCI 3220 OR Junior ENGR 2260 ENGR 2270 ENGR 2220 ENGR 3020 ENGR 2250 ENGR 2270 200 Hrs ENGR 1200 /ENGR 1210 STAT3100 ENGR 1200 /ENGR 1210 STAT3100 ENGR 4010 ENGR 2270 ENGR 2220 ENGR 3290 ENGR 1200 /ENGR 1210 STAT3100 Year 1 (Freshman) Year 2 (Sophomore) Year 3 (Junior) Year 4 (Senior) F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S F/S/U GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE GE PA PA PA PA CS CS CS CS CS SW SW SW SW SW MS MS MS MS MS MS MS AI AI AI AI AI LEGEND Pre-Req Co-Req * PR of MATH 2450 OR ([PR of MATH 1450 OR B in ENGR 1820] AND CR of MATH 2450) ** BIOL 1140, BIOL 1460, BIOL 1530/1530L, CHEM 1120/1120L (PR CHEM1020), CHEM 1150 & 115L (PR CHEM1020), PHYS 1130/1130L, PHYS2030 (PR PHYS2010), SCIE 1200, SCIE 2050 † ENGR 1240 + AUTOCAD can be used in place of ENGR 1220   ‡ Students taking ENGL1130E (4) MUST take ENGL1230E (4) F/S Offered multiple semesters: (F)all, (S)pring, S(U)mmer Counts for GE Credit Colors help visually match PR courses GE PA Programming, Algorithmic Core CS Computer Science Core SW Engineering Core SW AI AI/ML Concentration CY Cybersecurity Concentration Math/Science Core MS","CBU CS 26 27 4 Year Plan Start STAT3100 ML CONC\n\n(4) F/S Offered multiple semesters: (F)all, (S)pring, S(U)mmer Counts for GE Credit Colors help visually match PR courses GE PA Programming, Algorithmic Core CS Computer Science Core SW Engineering Core SW AI AI/ML Concentration CY Cybersecurity Concentration Math/Science Core MS ENGR1810/ MATH1150 ENGR1810/ MATH1150 Elective Unit (1) GE F/S Civic World (3) F/S GE **Misc. Or None Junior","BASAI Program Overview\n\nBASAI Program Overview Program Title Bachelor of Applied Science in Artificial Intelligence Program Description The Bachelor of Applied Science in Artificial Intelligence (BASAI) program at California Baptist University (CBU) is designed to prepare students to enter the workforce as successful Artificial Intelligence Specialists and Software Engineers. To effectively build and deploy modern AI, practitioners must be exceptional software developers first. Therefore, students learn the fundamentals of object-oriented programming, data structures, and algorithms through multiple languages, gaining the computational efficiency and logic required to train, optimize, and scale complex AI models. This strong algorithmic core serves as the springboard for the Software Engineering sequence. Here, students learn how to transition AI models from isolated scripts into production-ready enterprise applications. Coursework utilizes modern coding platforms and design paradigms to cover essential applied skills, including cloud platform and app design, mobile app development, information security, and software development lifecycles. This ensures students know how to securely deploy, host, and manage AI systems in the real world.","BASAI Program Overview\n\nand design paradigms to cover essential applied skills, including cloud platform and app design, mobile app development, information security, and software development lifecycles. This ensures students know how to securely deploy, host, and manage AI systems in the real world. A hallmark of the BASAI is its focus on applied problem-solving. Moving beyond pure theory, the program emphasizes the practical application of AI tools in real-world contexts. Students complete a Foundational AI sequence that provides significant learning and practice in the crucial subfields of search-based AI, shallow and deep learning, data mining, and natural language processing. The required mathematics core ensures students have the proper theoretical training in probability, statistics, and linear algebra to deeply understand, implement, and fine-tune machine learning algorithms. To further customize their learning experience, students take advanced applied AI electives. Drawing from rigorous graduate-level coursework, students explore emerging topics like parallel computing on GPUs, computer vision, reinforcement learning, AI-driven software development, the foundations of Large Language Models (LLMs) and agentic design, and other special topics in AI. This allows students to tackle specific domains and develop tailored AI solutions that address tangible problems.","BASAI Program Overview\n\nGPUs, computer vision, reinforcement learning, AI-driven software development, the foundations of Large Language Models (LLMs) and agentic design, and other special topics in AI. This allows students to tackle specific domains and develop tailored AI solutions that address tangible problems. Students leverage their coursework to identify a problem and create a significant, group-based capstone project. Through this project, they apply their technical skills to design, plan, and build a fully functional software and/or hardware system that integrates significant artificial intelligence capabilities. As part of the Bourns College of Engineering, all students also complete a supervised internship, gaining essential real-world experience before graduation. Graduates of the BASAI program are equipped with the technical skills and ethical foundation to drive technological innovation across industries. They are uniquely prepared for dynamic, high-impact roles such as Machine Learning Engineer, Applied AI Developer, MLOps Engineer, Data Engineer, and AI Solutions Architect. Curriculum Requirements (97-100 Units) General Education (15-18 units) The general education requirements will follow the curriculum set forth for other university programs. Some general education requirements will be met through specific Artificial Intelligence requirements. The following 15-18 units of general education are not met in the specific core and Artificial Intelligence requirements and must be taken: - ENGL 1130 Composition (3-4 units) - ENGL 1230 Intermediate Composition (3-4 units)","BASAI Program Overview\n\nthrough specific Artificial Intelligence requirements. The following 15-18 units of general education are not met in the specific core and Artificial Intelligence requirements and must be taken: - ENGL 1130 Composition (3-4 units) - ENGL 1230 Intermediate Composition (3-4 units) - Civic World Elective 1 of 2 (3 units) - Take from: WLDV1610, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, LAWT*, and more - Civic World Elective 2 of 2 (3 units) - Take from: WLDV1610, POLS*, HIST*, ECON*, LAWT*, and more - Cultural World Elective 1 of 1 (3 units) - Take from: WLDV1810, ENGL*, FILM*, HUMA*, PHIL*, Foreign Language, and more - *GNST 1000 First Year Experience (1 unit) *GNST 1000 is required for first year (non-transfer) students Lower Division Core Engineering Requirements (13 units) Take all of the following: - ENGR 1010 Introduction to Engineering (3 units) - ENGR 1020 Introduction Engineering Design (4 units) - ICST 1050 Introduction to Global Studies (3 units) - CHST 1300 New Testament Survey (3 units) *Junior/senior transfer students will complete ENGR 3010. All other students will complete ENGR 1010. **ENGR 1060 can be taken instead of ENGR 1020. ENGR 1240 can be taken instead of ENGR 1220. Upper Division Core Engineering Requirements (6 units) Take all of the following: - ENGR 3040 Leadership Cohort (1 unit) - ENGR 3060 Internship Preparation (1 unit)","BASAI Program Overview\n\ntaken instead of ENGR 1020. ENGR 1240 can be taken instead of ENGR 1220. Upper Division Core Engineering Requirements (6 units) Take all of the following: - ENGR 3040 Leadership Cohort (1 unit) - ENGR 3060 Internship Preparation (1 unit) - ENGR 4050 Internship Report and Presentation (1 unit) - CHST 3700 The Christian Faith (3 units) Programming and Algorithmic Requirements (9 units) Take all of the following: - ENGR 1200 Intro Computer Programming Python (3 units) - OR ENGR 1210 Intro Computer Programming C++ (3 units) - ENGR 2220 Software Engineering (3 units) - ENGR 2270 Data Structures and Analysis (3 units) Software Engineering Requirements (9 units) Take all of the following: - CSCI 3220 Mobile Application Development (3 units) - CSCI 3230 Cloud Computing and Application Development (3 units) - ENGR 3020 Engineering Design and Documentation (3 units) Math and Science Requirements (21 units) Take all of the following: - ENGR 1820 Intro Mathematics for Engr (3 units) - ENGR 1820L Intro Math for Engr App Lab (1 unit) - ENGR 2250 Discrete Structures (3 units) - MATH 2450 Analytical Geometry and Calculus I (4 units) - MATH 4030 Linear Algebra (3 units) - STAT 3100 Mathematical Statistics I (3 units) - OR ENGR 3050 Engineering Statistics (3 units) Complete 4 additional units from the following:","BASAI Program Overview\n\n- MATH 2450 Analytical Geometry and Calculus I (4 units) - MATH 4030 Linear Algebra (3 units) - STAT 3100 Mathematical Statistics I (3 units) - OR ENGR 3050 Engineering Statistics (3 units) Complete 4 additional units from the following: - BIOL 1140 General Botany w/ Lab (4 units) - BIOL 1460 General Biology I w/ Lab (4 units) - BIOL 1530 Anatomy and Physiology (3 units) - AND BIOL 1530L Anatomy and Physiology I Lab (1 unit) - CHEM 1120 Organic and Biochemistry for Health Science (3 units) - AND CHEM 1120L Orgnic and Biochemistry for Health Science Lab (1 unit) - CHEM 1150 General Chemistry I (3 units) - AND CHEM 1150L General Chemistry I Lab (1 unit) - PHYS 1130 Astronomy of the Solar System (3 units) - AND PHYS 1130L Astronomy of Solar System Lab (1 unit) - PHYS 2030 Physics for Engineers II w/ Lab (4 units) - SCIE 1200 Marine Science With Lab (4 units) - SCIE 2050 Intro to Forensic Science w/ Lab (4 units) Foundational AI Requirements (12 units) Take all of the following: - CSCI 4210 Artificial Intelligence (3 units) - CSCI 4220 Machine and Deep Learning (3 units) - CSCI 4230 Data Mining (3 units) - CSCI 4240 Natural Language Processing (3 units) Advanced Applied AI Electives (12 units)","BASAI Program Overview\n\nof the following: - CSCI 4210 Artificial Intelligence (3 units) - CSCI 4220 Machine and Deep Learning (3 units) - CSCI 4230 Data Mining (3 units) - CSCI 4240 Natural Language Processing (3 units) Advanced Applied AI Electives (12 units) Complete 12 additional units from the following: - CSCI 5400 Parallel Computing on GPUs (3 units) - CSCI 5500 Computer Vision (3 units) - CSCI 5510 Reinforcement Learning (3 units) - CSCI 5520 AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) - CSCI 5530 Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) - CSCI 5650 Special Topics in SW Engineering (3 units) - CSCI 5600 Big Data (3 units) NOTE: At least 4 unique courses from the above list will be offered each academic year on a rotating basis. Additional Degree Requirements - Complete a pre-approved and supervised internship of a minimum of 200 hours with a maximum of 1200 hours. - Complete the program assessment exam requirement.","CBU BASAI 26 27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\nCBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 1: 2025-2026, FALL 2025\nCourses taken in FALL 2025 (YEAR 1: 2025-2026) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 1010 - Engineering from Christian Worldview (3 units) [R]\n  ICST 1050 - Intro to Global Studies (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 1210 - Intro to Computer Programming in C++ (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R]\n  GNST 1000 - First Year Experience (1 units) [R]\n  ENGL 1130 - Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 1820 - Intro Math for Engr Appl (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 1: 2025-2026, SPRING 2026\nCourses taken in SPRING 2026 (YEAR 1: 2025-2026) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 1020 - Intro to Engineering Design (GE-CrW) (4 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2220 - Software Engineering (3 units) [R]\n  ENGL 1230 - Intermediate Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0003 - Cultural World (1 of 1) (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R]\n  MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calc 1 (GE-F) (4 units) [R]","CBU BASAI 26 27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n- Software Engineering (3 units) [R] ENGL 1230 - Intermediate Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R] GE 0003 - Cultural World (1 of 1) (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R] MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calc 1 (GE-F) (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 2: 2026-2027, FALL 2026\nCourses taken in FALL 2026 (YEAR 2: 2026-2027) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3060 - Internship Preparation (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2270 - Data Structures & Analysis (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 3220 - Mobile Application Development (3 units) [R]\n  CHST 1300 - Overview of the Bible (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2250 - Discrete Structures 1 (3 units) [R]\n  STAT 3100 / ENGR 3050 - Mathematical Statistics I (FA) or Engineering Stats (FA/SP) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI 26 27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n(3 units) [R] CHST 1300 - Overview of the Bible (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R] ENGR 2250 - Discrete Structures 1 (3 units) [R] STAT 3100 / ENGR 3050 - Mathematical Statistics I (FA) or Engineering Stats (FA/SP) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 2: 2026-2027, SPRING 2027\nCourses taken in SPRING 2027 (YEAR 2: 2026-2027) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3040 - Leadership Cohort (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  CSCI 3230 - Cloud Computing & App Development (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4210 - Artificial Intelligence (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0001 - Civic World (1 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R]\n  MATH 4030 - Linear Algebra (3 units) [R]\n  NSE - Natural Science Elective (Replace w/ Actual Course) (4 units) [R]","CBU BASAI 26 27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n- Artificial Intelligence (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R] GE 0001 - Civic World (1 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R] MATH 4030 - Linear Algebra (3 units) [R] NSE - Natural Science Elective (Replace w/ Actual Course) (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 3: 2027-2028, FALL 2027\nCourses taken in FALL 2027 (YEAR 3: 2027-2028) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 4050 - Internship Report & Presentation (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4220 - Machine  & Deep Learning (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4230 - Datamining (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5520 - (GRAD) AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5590 - Special Topics in AI - Professional AI-Based SW Architectures (from Startup to Enterprise) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0002 - Civic World (2 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI 26 27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n5520 - (GRAD) AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) [R] CSCI 5590 - Special Topics in AI - Professional AI-Based SW Architectures (from Startup to Enterprise) (3 units) [R] GE 0002 - Civic World (2 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 3: 2027-2028, SPRING 2028\nCourses taken in SPRING 2028 (YEAR 3: 2027-2028) of the CBU BASAI 26-27 3 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3020 - Engineering Design & Documentation (GE-SW) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4240 - Natural Language Processing (NLP) (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5500 - (GRAD) Computer Vision (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5530 - (GRAD) Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) [R]\n  CHST 3700 - The Chrsitian Faith (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\nCBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 1: 2025-2026, FALL 2025\nCourses taken in FALL 2025 (YEAR 1: 2025-2026) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 1010 - Engineering from Christian Worldview (3 units) [R]\n  ICST 1050 - Intro to Global Studies (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 1210 - Intro to Computer Programming in C++ (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R]\n  GNST 1000 - First Year Experience (1 units) [R]\n  ENGL 1130 - Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 1820 - Intro Math for Engr Appl (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 1: 2025-2026, SPRING 2026\nCourses taken in SPRING 2026 (YEAR 1: 2025-2026) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 1020 - Intro to Engineering Design (GE-CrW) (4 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2220 - Software Engineering (3 units) [R]\n  ENGL 1230 - Intermediate Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0003 - Cultural World (1 of 1) (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R]\n  MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calc 1 (GE-F) (4 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n- Software Engineering (3 units) [R] ENGL 1230 - Intermediate Composition (3/4) (GE-F) (3 units) [R] GE 0003 - Cultural World (1 of 1) (GE-CuW) (3 units) [R] MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calc 1 (GE-F) (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 2: 2026-2027, FALL 2026\nCourses taken in FALL 2026 (YEAR 2: 2026-2027) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3060 - Internship Preparation (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2270 - Data Structures & Analysis (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 3220 - Mobile Application Development (3 units) [R]\n  CHST 1300 - Overview of the Bible (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]\n  ENGR 2250 - Discrete Structures 1 (3 units) [R]\n  STAT 3100 / ENGR 3050 - Mathematical Statistics I (FA) or Engineering Stats (FA/SP) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n(3 units) [R] CHST 1300 - Overview of the Bible (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R] ENGR 2250 - Discrete Structures 1 (3 units) [R] STAT 3100 / ENGR 3050 - Mathematical Statistics I (FA) or Engineering Stats (FA/SP) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 2: 2026-2027, SPRING 2027\nCourses taken in SPRING 2027 (YEAR 2: 2026-2027) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3040 - Leadership Cohort (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  CSCI 3230 - Cloud Computing & App Development (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4210 - Artificial Intelligence (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0001 - Civic World (1 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R]\n  MATH 4030 - Linear Algebra (3 units) [R]\n  NSE - Natural Science Elective (Replace w/ Actual Course) (4 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\n- Artificial Intelligence (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R] GE 0001 - Civic World (1 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R] MATH 4030 - Linear Algebra (3 units) [R] NSE - Natural Science Elective (Replace w/ Actual Course) (4 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 3: 2027-2028, FALL 2027\nCourses taken in FALL 2027 (YEAR 3: 2027-2028) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 4050 - Internship Report & Presentation (GE-SW) (1 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4220 - Machine  & Deep Learning (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4230 - Datamining (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5520 - AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5590 - Special Topics in AI - Professional AI-Based SW Architectures (from Startup to Enterprise) (3 units) [R]\n  GE 0002 - Civic World (2 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\nCSCI 5520 - AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) [R] CSCI 5590 - Special Topics in AI - Professional AI-Based SW Architectures (from Startup to Enterprise) (3 units) [R] GE 0002 - Civic World (2 of 2) (GE-CiW) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 3: 2027-2028, SPRING 2028\nCourses taken in SPRING 2028 (YEAR 3: 2027-2028) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  ENGR 3020 - Engineering Design & Documentation (GE-SW) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4240 - Natural Language Processing (NLP) (AI/ML Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4310 - (MSAI) Info Security and Computer Forensics (CS Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5500 - Computer Vision (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5530 - Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) [R]\n  CHST 3700 - The Chrsitian Faith (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R]","CBU BASAI MSAI 26 27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820\n\nInfo Security and Computer Forensics (CS Conc.) (3 units) [R] CSCI 5500 - Computer Vision (3 units) [R] CSCI 5530 - Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) [R] CHST 3700 - The Chrsitian Faith (GE-BTC) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 4: 2028-2029, FALL 2028\nCourses taken in FALL 2028 (YEAR 4: 2028-2029) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  CSCI 4330 - (MSAI) Cyber Security Incident Response & Recovery (CS Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5400 - (MSAI) Parallel Computing on GPUs (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5590b - (MSAI) Special Topics AI - Alternative Topic (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5900 - (MSAI) Research, Project Methodology & Design (First offering 26-27) (3 units) [R] CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820 - YEAR 4: 2028-2029, SPRING 2029\nCourses taken in SPRING 2029 (YEAR 4: 2028-2029) of the CBU BASAI MSAI 26-27 3p1 Year Program Start ENGR1820:\n  CSCI 4320 - (MSAI) Network Traffic Analysis (CS Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 4340 - (MSAI) Secure System Principles (CS Conc.) (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5510 - (MSAI) Reinforcement Learning (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5600 - (MSAI) Big Data (3 units) [R]\n  CSCI 5910 - (MSAI) Capstone Project (First offering 26-27) (3 units) [R]","CBU CoE Internship Graduation Handout 26 27\n\nCBU CoE Internship Graduation Handout 26 27 Internship Requirement for Graduation\n2026–2027 To fully meet the internship graduation requirement, students must complete Pre & Post Internship Requirements: STAGE 1: Pre-Internship – Get Approved Goal: Get your internship approved before or at the start of your internship. Step 1: Secure an Internship –Related to major with professional-level experience. Step 2: Complete the Internship Application – Obtain your supervisor’s signature. Step 3: Submit Application Online – Upload signed supervisor acknowledgement using the portal. What Happens Next - Confirmation email sent after submission - Application routed for Department Chair approval - Notification sent within two weeks - After completing 200 hours, post-internship instructions are emailed STAGE 2: Post-Internship – Complete and Submit Goal: Prepare & submit final requirements: Post Internship Submissions Step 1: Student Evaluation – Complete and submit via portal. Step 2: Reflection – Brief summary of your experience. No employer review required. Step 3: Executive Summary – Formal summary reviewed and acknowledged by your supervisor. Questions? Contact: coeint01@calbaptist.edu","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nMSAI CBU Program v1.6 Program Description The Master of Science in Artificial Intelligence (MSAI) at California Baptist University offers an innovative curriculum designed to empower professionals to harness the transformative power of artificial intelligence within their specific fields of expertise. With a dynamic core curriculum that includes topics like Computer Vision, Parallel Computing on GPUs, Reinforcement Learning, AI-Driven Software Development, and Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design, this program moves beyond pure theory to emphasize the practical application of AI tools in real-world contexts. A hallmark of the MSAI program is its focus on applied problem-solving through open-ended coursework. Recognizing that innovation happens at the intersection of AI and domain expertise, the program encourages students to customize their learning experience. Students are invited to bring their own datasets and unique challenges (whether from their current workplaces, specific industries, or personal areas of interest) to develop tailored AI solutions that address tangible problems. ~~To further accommodate diverse professional schedules and learning styles, the program features a highly flexible hybrid delivery model. Most courses are designed to be accessible in-classroom, synchronously online, or asynchronously via lecture recordings, allowing students to effectively balance their education with career demands and personal commitments.~~","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nand learning styles, the program features a highly flexible hybrid delivery model. Most courses are designed to be accessible in-classroom, synchronously online, or asynchronously via lecture recordings, allowing students to effectively balance their education with career demands and personal commitments.~~ Students have the flexibility to deepen their expertise through specific breadth and depth areas, including Foundational AI & Machine Learning, Statistics & Data Science, Cybersecurity and Applied AI alongside required coursework in Cloud Computing. Through hands-on projects, rigorous research methodologies, and exposure to real-world applications, students will build a strong technical foundation and become ethical leaders capable of integrating intelligent systems into any sector. Whether you are a computer scientist looking to specialize or a professional seeking to drive innovation in your industry through technology, the MSAI program at CBU provides the flexible framework, tools, and support to help you succeed. Program Requirements Required Courses Students are required to take a total of 30 units of coursework, spread across 4 categories: 1.  Applied AI Core Courses (12 units): These courses serve as the heart of the program, designed to provide rigorous, applied instruction in modern AI technologies. Students will move beyond theory to implement practical AI solutions for complex problems of interest.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nApplied AI Core Courses (12 units): These courses serve as the heart of the program, designed to provide rigorous, applied instruction in modern AI technologies. Students will move beyond theory to implement practical AI solutions for complex problems of interest. 2.  Cloud Computing Core Courses (3 units): Because the majority of modern AI deployment occurs in the cloud, this requirement ensures students have foundational familiarity with major platforms. Students will gain the skills necessary to navigate and leverage cloud infrastructure to support scalable applications. 3.  Breadth/Depth Courses (9 units): This category exposes students to distinct technical challenges in fields like machine learning, cybersecurity, GIS, and data science, broadening their problem-solving scope. By exploring these diverse areas, students discover new domains where they can apply their AI and machine learning expertise. 4.  Research/Project Courses (6 units): This capstone sequence guides students through the complete lifecycle of technical innovation, from researching the state-of-the-art to project planning. It culminates in the creation of a working proof-of-concept that solves a specific problem within a domain of the student's choice. NOTE: Undergraduate courses that are taken as part of a CBU undergraduate degree may NOT be taken as part of a CBU graduate degree. Applied AI Core Courses (12 units) Students must take 4 core courses (12 units total), from the following list:","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nthat are taken as part of a CBU undergraduate degree may NOT be taken as part of a CBU graduate degree. Applied AI Core Courses (12 units) Students must take 4 core courses (12 units total), from the following list: - CSCI 5520 – AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) - OR CSCI 5400 - Parallel Computing on GPUs (3 units) - CSCI 5530 – Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) - OR CSCI 5510 – Reinforcement Learning (3 units) - CSCI 5500 – Computer Vision (3 units) - OR CSCI 5600 – Big Data (3 units) - CSCI 5590 – Topics in AI (3 units) NOTE: Not all core courses are guaranteed to be offered each semester or year; thus, while at least 4 of these courses (one from each set of options) will be offered every year, there may be individual courses that are not offered during your tenure. Cloud Computing Core Courses (3 units) Students must take 1 cloud computing course (3 units total), from the following list: - CSCI 3230 - Cloud Computing & Application Development – Offered Every Spring - CTEC 4200 - Cloud Computing - Existing Course – Offered Every Spring (Online Only)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\ntake 1 cloud computing course (3 units total), from the following list: - CSCI 3230 - Cloud Computing & Application Development – Offered Every Spring - CTEC 4200 - Cloud Computing - Existing Course – Offered Every Spring (Online Only) NOTE: Students who took Cloud Computing as part of their CBU undergraduate degree can/must substitute this requirement for an additional Applied AI Core (as available) or Breadth/Depth Area course that is not meeting another program requirement. Breadth/Depth Courses (9 units) Students must take 3 core courses (9 units total), which can be taken from a single breadth/depth area, or across any combination of breadth/depth areas: - Breadth/Depth Area 1: Foundational AI & Machine Learning - CSCI 4210 - Artificial Intelligence (3 units) – Every Spring - CSCI 4220 - Machine & Deep Learning (3 units) – Every Fall - CSCI 4230 - Data Mining (3 units) – Every Fall - CSCI 4240 - Natural Language Processing (3 units) – Every Spring - Breadth/Depth Area 2: Statistics & Data Science - STAT 3600 - Data Visualization – EVEN Springs - STAT 3620 - Advanced Statistical Computing – EVEN Falls - STAT 3640 - Applied Statistical Methodology – ODD Springs - Breadth/Depth Area 3: Cybersecurity - CSCI 4310 – Info Security and Computer Forensics (3 units) – Every Spring","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nSprings - STAT 3620 - Advanced Statistical Computing – EVEN Falls - STAT 3640 - Applied Statistical Methodology – ODD Springs - Breadth/Depth Area 3: Cybersecurity - CSCI 4310 – Info Security and Computer Forensics (3 units) – Every Spring - CSCI 4320 - Network Traffic Analysis (3 units) – Every Spring - CSCI 4330 - Cyber Security Incident Response & Recovery (3 units) – Every Fall - CSCI 4340 - Secure System Principles (3 units) – Every Spring - Breadth/Depth Area 4: Applied AI - CSCI 5400 - Parallel Computing on GPUs (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5500 - Computer Vision (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5510 - Reinforcement Learning (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5520 - AI-Driven Software Development (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5530 - Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5590 - Topics in AI (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5600 - Big Data (3 units) – As Offered","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nAs Offered - CSCI 5530 - Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5590 - Topics in AI (3 units) – As Offered - CSCI 5600 - Big Data (3 units) – As Offered NOTE: Once students have fulfilled their 12 units of Applied AI Core Courses, they may take additional CSCI5XXX Applied AI Core courses, as available, to fulfill Breadth/Depth elective requirements (Breadth/Depth Area 4: Applied AI); courses will NOT be double counted for the Applied AI Core and Breadth/Depth courses. However, CSCI 5590 (Topics in AI) may be taken an additional time for Breadth/Depth credit as long as the topic of the course has changed from when it was taken as an Applied AI Core Course fulfillment. Research & Project Courses (6 units) Students must take both research and project courses (6 units total), including: - CSCI 5900 - Research, Project Methodology & Design – Every Semester - CSCI 5910 - Capstone Project – Every Semester Pre-requisites Unconditional Pre-requisites (Program Level) Several unconditional pre-requisites are required at the program level and must be satisfied before entering any courses required by the degree: - Statistics Course - STAT 3100 - Math Statistics I (or similar) OR - ENGR 305 - Engineering Statistics (or similar) OR","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nare required at the program level and must be satisfied before entering any courses required by the degree: - Statistics Course - STAT 3100 - Math Statistics I (or similar) OR - ENGR 305 - Engineering Statistics (or similar) OR - STAT 1440 - Introduction to Statistics (or similar) - Intro Programming Course (or similar) - ENGR 1200 - Introduction to Programming in Python (or similar) OR - ENGR 1210 - Introduction to Programming in C++ (or similar) OR - Relevant and demonstrable programming experience - In a case where no formal coursework in computer programming is evident on a transcript, the program director will ascertain proficiency or pre-requisite coursework for acceptance into the program. NOTE: CBU offers intro programming and stats courses both Fall and Winter semesters. Conditional Pre-requisites (Course Level) The conditional pre-requisites are at the course level and must be taken before taking those specific courses; this is in addition to the statistics and intro programming courses required to take any course in the program. Courses with specific pre-requisites are listed below: - CSCI 4210 - Artificial Intelligence - ENGR 2270 - Data Structures and Analysis (or similar) OR - CSCI 3120 - Algorithms (or similar) - STAT 3600 - Data Visualization - MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calculus 1 (or similar) OR","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\n4210 - Artificial Intelligence - ENGR 2270 - Data Structures and Analysis (or similar) OR - CSCI 3120 - Algorithms (or similar) - STAT 3600 - Data Visualization - MATH 2450 - Analytical Geometry & Calculus 1 (or similar) OR - CSCI 5910 - Capstone Project - CSCI 5900 - Research, Project Methodology & Design NOTE: Courses with conditional pre-requisites in the breadth/depth area are NOT required, and thus, these pre-requisites can be avoided by selecting different breadth/depth courses. Grad Course Descriptions & Organization CSCI Graduate Breadth/Depth Areas M.S. level graduate courses (CSCI 5XXX) at CBU are generally organized into one of the following areas, based on their course numbering: - CSCI 53XX – Cybersecurity & Information Assurance - The Shield – Securing the digital landscape against evolving threats. - This area focuses on the principles and practices of defending systems, networks, and data. Students will learn to design secure architectures, analyze vulnerabilities, and implement robust cryptographic protocols to ensure privacy and integrity in an increasingly hostile digital environment. - CSCI 54XX – High-Performance & Distributed Systems - The Infrastructure – Designing the backbone of modern computing.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nto design secure architectures, analyze vulnerabilities, and implement robust cryptographic protocols to ensure privacy and integrity in an increasingly hostile digital environment. - CSCI 54XX – High-Performance & Distributed Systems - The Infrastructure – Designing the backbone of modern computing. - This area explores the architecture of massive-scale systems. Students will move beyond basic software development to master the hardware-software interface, learning how to engineer distributed networks, parallel processing pipelines, and high-performance computing environments that power modern clouds and supercomputers. - CSCI 55XX – Artificial Intelligence & Intelligent Systems - The Intelligence – Building the \"brain\" of the system. - This area encompasses the algorithms and models that enable machines to reason, perceive, and learn. Students will dive deep into modern AI, covering everything from computer vision and reinforcement learning to the design of autonomous agents and Large Language Models (LLMs), preparing them to build systems that can solve complex, open-ended problems. - CSCI 56XX – Data Engineering & Analytics - The Data – Managing the lifeblood of the digital economy.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nthe design of autonomous agents and Large Language Models (LLMs), preparing them to build systems that can solve complex, open-ended problems. - CSCI 56XX – Data Engineering & Analytics - The Data – Managing the lifeblood of the digital economy. - This area bridges the gap between raw data and actionable insight. Students will learn the end-to-end lifecycle of data, mastering the engineering required to build scalable data pipelines (ETL/ELT) and the analytical techniques needed to mine, visualize, and interpret massive datasets using modern cloud tools like BigQuery and Spark. - CSCI 59XX – Research & Applied Innovation - The Innovation – Transforming concepts into reality. - This area encompasses the methodologies of applied research, project design, and technical implementation. It integrates the technical pillars of the program (i.e., Infrastructure, Intelligence and Data), challenging students to identify real-world problems and engineer comprehensive solutions. From rigorous state-of-the-art analysis to agile project execution, students demonstrate their mastery by delivering professional-grade, AI-driven prototypes and applications for complex, real-world domains. Applied AI Core Courses 1.  CSCI 5400 - Parallel Computing on GPUs - New Course (MSCS)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nsolutions. From rigorous state-of-the-art analysis to agile project execution, students demonstrate their mastery by delivering professional-grade, AI-driven prototypes and applications for complex, real-world domains. Applied AI Core Courses 1. CSCI 5400 - Parallel Computing on GPUs - New Course (MSCS) This course focuses on the principles and practices of general-purpose parallel programming using Graphics Processing Units (GPUs). Students will examine GPU architectures, parallel computing models, and techniques for optimizing performance using frameworks such as CUDA. Topics include data parallelism, memory management, and the design of scalable algorithms for high-performance computing tasks. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply parallelization fundamentals to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 2.  CSCI 5500 – Computer Vision - New Course (MSCS) This course explores the practical application of computer vision, focusing on the development of intelligent systems that interpret and analyze visual data. Students will study and implement modern deep learning architectures and techniques, such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), real-time object detection frameworks (e.g., YOLO), and semantic segmentation. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply computer vision fundamentals to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 3.  CSCI 5510 – Reinforcement Learning – New Course (MSCSAAI)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\ncourse culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply computer vision fundamentals to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 3. CSCI 5510 – Reinforcement Learning – New Course (MSCSAAI) This course provides a practical introduction to reinforcement learning (RL), where students learn to build intelligent agents that adapt and make decisions within dynamic environments. Students will implement and experiment with foundational and modern RL algorithms, such as Deep Q-Networks (DQN), Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), and Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF). The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply reinforcement learning fundamentals to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 4.  CSCI 5520 – AI-Driven Software Development – New Course (MSCSAAI)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nculminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply reinforcement learning fundamentals to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 4. CSCI 5520 – AI-Driven Software Development – New Course (MSCSAAI) This course empowers software engineers to leverage artificial intelligence throughout the entire software development lifecycle (SDLC). Students will learn modern techniques for AI-assisted ideation and rapid UI/UX prototyping, as well as AI enhancements in project management and version control. A major emphasis is placed on agentic coding and IDE integration, where students master workflows using AI coding assistants to accelerate development while maintaining architectural control and code quality. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply these AI-driven methodologies to build a rapid software prototype for an open-ended problem of their choice.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\naccelerate development while maintaining architectural control and code quality. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply these AI-driven methodologies to build a rapid software prototype for an open-ended problem of their choice. 5.  CSCI 5530 – Foundations of LLMs and Agentic Design – New Course (MSCSAAI)\n    This course moves beyond the hype of Large Language Models (LLMs) to explore their practical architecture, capabilities, and limitations without focusing on model training. After establishing a solid theoretical foundation, the curriculum shifts to Agentic Design, where students learn to architect autonomous systems that can reason, plan, and execute tasks. Using modern orchestration frameworks like LangGraph, students will build stateful agents equipped with custom tools to solve complex, multi-step problems. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to design and implement a robust multi-agent system to address an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific domain challenge. 6.  CSCI 5590 – Topics in Applied AI – New Course (MSCSAAI)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nproject during the final weeks, allowing students to design and implement a robust multi-agent system to address an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific domain challenge. 6. CSCI 5590 – Topics in Applied AI – New Course (MSCSAAI) This course provides an in-depth exploration of emerging trends, advanced methodologies, and specialized domains within applied artificial intelligence not covered in the standard curriculum. Designed to adapt to the rapidly evolving AI landscape, the course content rotates to reflect the specific research expertise of the instructor and current industry demands. Potential topics may include Natural Language Processing (NLP), generative media, AI for healthcare, robotics, or ethical AI governance. Students will engage with state-of-the-art literature and tools relevant to the selected topic. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply these specialized concepts to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 7.  CSCI 5600 – Big Data – Existing Course (MSCS)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\ncourse culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to apply these specialized concepts to an open-ended problem of their choice or a specific dataset of interest. 7. CSCI 5600 – Big Data – Existing Course (MSCS) This course bridges the gap between data analysis and data engineering, covering the tools and methodologies for managing large-scale datasets. Students will learn to design and construct scalable data pipelines using distributed processing frameworks like Spark and modern cloud data warehouses like Google BigQuery. Topics include ETL/ELT processes, efficient data ingestion, SQL for big data, and the architecture of real-time vs. batch processing systems. The course culminates in a major project during the final weeks, allowing students to build an end-to-end data pipeline or analytics solution for an open-ended problem of their choice. Research/Project Courses 1.  CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) This course prepares students to identify and architect solutions for complex, real-world technical challenges. Moving beyond traditional theory, the curriculum focuses on applied research methodologies, including defining actionable problem statements and conducting a rigorous analysis of the state-of-the-art (SOTA) in AI and software technology. Topics include technical feasibility analysis, ethical considerations in AI deployment, solution architecture, and technical writing. Students will develop a comprehensive project proposal and design a roadmap for their capstone implementation.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nanalysis of the state-of-the-art (SOTA) in AI and software technology. Topics include technical feasibility analysis, ethical considerations in AI deployment, solution architecture, and technical writing. Students will develop a comprehensive project proposal and design a roadmap for their capstone implementation. 2.  CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) In this course, students execute the proposal developed in CSCI 5900 to deliver a fully functional, professional-grade solution. Students will apply advanced techniques—such as AI/ML integration, cloud architecture, or data engineering—to build a robust proof-of-concept or market-ready prototype. The experience mimics an industry environment, emphasizing agile project management, iterative development, and the professional demonstration of the final product to stakeholders. Student Course Schedule This section presents schedules for students under various scenarios. 6 units/semester will be considered “full time”. Classes will not be offered regularly over the summer, and thus, course work is expected to be completed in the Fall and Spring semesters. The initial plan is for the following course offerings: - Fall Only - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 2 (3 units) - Spring Only - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nAI Core Course 2 (3 units) - Spring Only - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course 4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development - Fall/Spring Always - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project - Fall or Spring - Breadth/Depth areas courses are offered in the Fall or Spring, depending on the course, and typically will not be offered every semester Applied AI Core Courses will be offered each semester on a rotating basis based on faculty availability, expertise and other internal factors; two unique Applied AI Core Courses will be offered in both the Fall and Spring semesters such that 4 unique courses will be offered in any academic year. Thus, incoming students can always complete 4 unique Applied AI Core Courses within their first 2 semesters, regardless of which semester they start. Given that the CSCI 5900 – CSCI 5910 sequence is meant to be a 2-semester sequence, these courses would be offered both Fall and Spring so that a student could always take this 2-course sequence during their last 2 semesters in the program, regardless of what semester they start.","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nis meant to be a 2-semester sequence, these courses would be offered both Fall and Spring so that a student could always take this 2-course sequence during their last 2 semesters in the program, regardless of what semester they start. Given that there are 13+ breadth/depth options, no special consideration is given to ensuring that a student can take any combination; however, it is likely that students will be able to take any combination, regardless of which semester they begin. NOTE: The schedules below always show a Fall semester start but could easily be adjusted for a Spring semester start. 2 Year Plans (4 semesters) It is likely that the vast majority of students will be international students desiring to complete their degree over a 2-year (4 semester) period. Well-Prepared Student w/o ESL (30 units) The “well-prepared” student meets any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses) and/or is determined to be able to jump straight into required course curriculum. - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (9 units):","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\n- Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - MSCS Semester 3 - Fall (6 units): - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 4 - Spring (6 units): - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) Well-Prepared Student w/ ESL (33 units) The “well-prepared” student meets any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses) and/or is determined to be able to jump straight into required course curriculum. - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - ESLG 094 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nunits) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - ESLG 094 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - MSCS Semester 3 - Fall (9 units): - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 4 - Spring (6 units): - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) Under-Prepared Student w/o ESL (Worst-Case) (36 units) The “under-prepared” worst-case student does NOT meet any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses). - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (6 units): - PR 1 - ENGR 1200 – Intro to Programming in Python (or similar) - PR 2 - STAT 3100 - Math Stats I (or similar) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nMSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 3 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 4 - Spring (9 units): - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) Under-Prepared Student w/ ESL (Worst-Case) (39 units) The “under-prepared” worst-case student does NOT meet any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses). - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (9 units): - PR 1 - ENGR 1200 – Intro to Programming in Python (or similar) - PR 2 - STAT 3100 - Math Stats I (or similar) - ESLG 0940 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nsimilar) - ESLG 0940 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 3 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 4 - Spring (9 units): - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) 1.5 Year Plans (3 semesters) A highly motivated, non-CBU domestic student may desire and be able to complete their degree over a 1.5-year (3 semester) period. Well-Prepared Student w/o ESL (30 units) The “well-prepared” student meets any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses) and/or is determined to be able to jump straight into required course curriculum. - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nto be able to jump straight into required course curriculum. - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (9 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Fall (9 units): - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) Well-Prepared Student w/ ESL (33 units) The “well-prepared” student meets any program pre-requisites (this plan does not consider additional/specific course pre-requisites for non-required courses) and/or is determined to be able to jump straight into required course curriculum. - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - ESLG 0940 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units):","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nAI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - ESLG 0940 – Advanced Academic Research (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Fall (9 units): - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) 1 Year Plans (2 semesters) Students bringing in at least 6-units of transferable credit, students in the 4+1 program and/or extremely high-performing students may be able to complete the MSCS program in 2 full-time semesters. NOTE: The case of a student bringing in 6-units of transferable credit is not explicitly shown below but is similar to the 4+1 plan that is shown. 4+1 Student (Advanced Undergrad Students) (30 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nMSCS program in 2 full-time semesters. NOTE: The case of a student bringing in 6-units of transferable credit is not explicitly shown below but is similar to the 4+1 plan that is shown. 4+1 Student (Advanced Undergrad Students) (30 units) Exceptional undergraduate students will be considered and accepted into the “4+1” program as part of a 5-year BS+MS degree combo program. This course schedule assumes and requires that the 4+1 student can complete their BSCS degree requirements in four years, and also assumes the student completes at least an additional 6 units of courses as an undergrad that can be applied to their MSCS degree requirements (these units cannot count toward their BSCS degree). It does not matter which courses are completed and the 4+1 student is free to choose any courses in which they meet the pre-requisites for and/or in which they are approved by the department or program chair. For the sake of example, this guide assumes two “arbitrary” courses are completed during the student’s first 4 years as an undergraduate: - BSCS Undergraduate Years – Fall/Spring (6 units): - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nundergraduate: - BSCS Undergraduate Years – Fall/Spring (6 units): - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - *CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - *Replace with B/D Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (12 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units) Exceptional Students (Advanced Undergrad Students) (30 units) Exceptional undergraduate students with a remarkable undergraduate record may be able to complete the program in 2 full-time semesters with no units being transferred in toward the degree. These students should have a strong GPA in a Computer Science (or closely related) major, thus having a strong foundation to sustain the increased unit load: - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (15 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units)","MSAI CBU Program v1.6\n\nhaving a strong foundation to sustain the increased unit load: - MSCS Semester 1 - Fall (15 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #1 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #2 (3 units) - B/D Course #1 (3 units) - B/D Course #2 (3 units) - CSCI 5900 – Research, Project Methodology & Design (3 units) - MSCS Semester 2 - Spring (15 units): - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5XXX – Applied AI Core Course #4 (3 units) - CSCI 3230 – Cloud Computing & Application Development (3 units) - B/D Course #3 (3 units) - CSCI 5910 – Capstone Project (3 units)"],"uris":null,"metadatas":[{"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/","index":0,"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/"},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/","source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/academics/","index":1},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/","index":0,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/"},{"index":1,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/","title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/"},{"index":2,"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/","source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/"},{"index":3,"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/","source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/admissions/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/","index":0,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/life-at-cbu/"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/life-at-cbu/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/","index":1},{"index":2,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/life-at-cbu/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/life at cbu/"},{"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/","index":0,"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/"},{"index":1,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/","title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/"},{"index":2,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/","title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/"},{"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/","index":3,"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/about/"},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":0},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":1},{"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":2},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":3,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/"},{"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":4},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":5,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/"},{"title":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/","index":6,"source":"https://www.calbaptist.edu/engineering/"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-computer-science/","index":0,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/"},{"index":1,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-computer-science/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-computer-science/","index":2},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-computer-science/","index":3},{"index":4,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-computer-science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor computer science","index":0,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor-computer-science"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor-computer-engineering","index":0,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor computer engineering"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/","index":0},{"index":1,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/","index":2},{"index":3,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/"},{"index":4,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/"},{"index":5,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master of science computer science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/master-of-science-computer-science/","index":6},{"index":0,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor data sciences","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/minor-data-sciences"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/","index":0,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/"},{"index":1,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/","index":2},{"index":3,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/","index":4,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-data-science/","index":5,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science data science/"},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-computer-engineering/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","index":0},{"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-computer-engineering/","title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","index":1},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-computer-engineering/","index":2},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-computer-engineering/","index":3},{"index":4,"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-computer-engineering/"},{"title":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor of science electrical and computer engineering/","index":5,"source":"https://calbaptist.edu/academics/programs/bachelor-of-science-electrical-and-co